p_8a50e0f6b709108a,531,60,post_entry,bess_capex,500.224690337120030624831946491062295,0,500.224690337120030624831946491062295,1.00,0.0000,18007392,,,,\\r\\np_8a50e0f6b709108a,532,60,post_entry,bess_maintenance,500.224690337120030624831946491062295,-0.0821684587813620071684587813620071685,500.142521878338668617663487709700288,1.00,0.0000,18007392,,,,\\r\\np_8a50e0f6b709108a,533,60,post_entry,debt_interest,500.142521878338668617663487709700288,0.000000,500.142521878338668617663487709700288,1.00,0.0000,18007392,,,,\\r\\np_8a50e0f6b709108a,534,60,post_entry,back_operating_cash_and_deposit_interest,500.142521878338668617663487709700288,7.94706922233271729880657463610325114,508.089591100671385916470062345803539,1.00,0.0000,18007392,,,,\\r\\n\"","\"information_cutoff\"","\"price_date\"","\"market_cap_crore\"","\"decisive_reason\"","\"model_counts\"","\"primary_cases\"","\"complete_cash_evidence\"","\"Strong EPC growth has not converted to cash; entry funding and BSE executable liquidity remain unresolved. BESS is finite, capital intensive and only conditionally grant supported.\"","\"cash_survival\"","\"terminal_self_funding_survival\"","\"Both packages independently reconstruct complete canonical/model, cash-event and valuation outputs from authored inputs and code; full additionally includes those bulky generated files.\"","\"# Rajesh Power Services Limited\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Hold — unresolved funding and tradability. BQS 5.9/10; GQS 5.0/10; IAS NS.** Rajesh Power is a fast-growing electrical transmission and distribution EPC contractor, not a mature utility with recurring cash already in hand. Qualifications, execution scale and Gujarat utility relationships are real strengths. The decisive weakness is conversion: comparable FY25–26 operating cash flow was **negative ₹68.12 crore** against ₹239.83 crore profit. Retentions, receivables and guarantee collateral absorb capital while the proposed owned BESS adds a different, capital-intensive risk. At the secondary-corroborated ₹821.85 close on 19 October 2026, the conditional base model returns 12.67%, below the 15% analytical hurdle. That is a scenario, not a scoreable forecast of current available funding. The bear breaches cash before the investment date, so no ordinary return is shown. This is an **execution-led infrastructure growth thesis with financing risk**, not a proven compounder. Verified collections, drawable facilities, project financing and exchange liquidity could change the conclusion; a low P/E alone cannot. [FY26 annual report](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf), [July rating](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html).\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n| Item | Basis |\\n|---|---|\\n| Legal issuer | Rajesh Power Services Limited; CIN L31300GJ2010PLC059536; ISIN INE0VN801010 |\\n| Listing | BSE SME, code 544291, symbol RAJESH. NSE interoperability master symbol RAJESH$ is **not evidence of an NSE listing**. |\\n| Information cutoff | 20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata. Research retrieved 23 October, using only disclosures available by the cutoff. |\\n| Price | ₹821.85 on 19 October 2026, secondary cross-check, not a verified executable exchange quote |\\n| Shares / market cap | 18,007,392 fully paid shares; market cap ₹1,479.94 crore. Current master lot 100 shares. |\\n| Financial scope | FY26 audited consolidated Ind AS; FY25 restated comparative. Older standalone/IPO-restated series explicitly separated. No H1 FY27 cash results assumed. |\\n| Horizon / hurdle | Five years from 19 October 2026 to 19 October 2031; 15% nominal annualized before investor tax, tested at 12% and 18%. |\\n| Liquidity reference | ₹5 lakh analytical reference, not a personal allocation recommendation. Rounded down to six lots: 600 shares, ₹493,110 before costs. |\\n\\n| Gate | Result | Evidence and consequence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Conditional | Clean audit opinions do not remove connected-party exposure, loan-term inconsistencies or allocation questions. No substantiated severe misconduct; no GOV≤3 override. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Conditional | Latest audited Ind AS statements are usable. Preserve original CFO vintages and transition bridge rather than invent a uniform series. No established material misstatement requiring a Fail. |\\n| G3 Funding / survival | **Hold** | March cash is not October cash. Current drawable terms, renewal, guarantees and BESS financial closure are unverified. Conditional scenario survival is not committed finance. No finding of actual default, so no mechanical IAS≤3 funding cap. |\\n| G4 Tradability | **Hold** | Official BSE 20/60-session tape, spread and restrictions were not obtained. Missing rows are ND, never zero-volume observations. No unsupported suspension claim. |\\n\\nLiquidity capacity remains ND at **2%, 5% and 10% participation** because the conservative denominator, minimum of 60-session mean and median traded value including zero-trade days, is missing. The 20-session mean/median, zero-trade count and spreads are also ND. No exit-days score is fabricated from one print.\\n\\nConfidence: **High** on latest audited P&L, capital and original audit opinions; **Medium** on economically comparable longer-term growth, capital efficiency and order execution; **Low** on actual entry-date unrestricted cash, future BESS funding and executable liquidity. Promoter holding was 72.70%, with no reported pledge in the March 2026 filing; that is dated evidence, not a guarantee of October status. [Shareholding filing](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Shareholding%20Pattern%20-%2031.03.2026.pdf), [official security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz).\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\n| Measure | Exact raw | Displayed final | Evidence coverage | Override / missingness |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| BQS | 5.88 | 5.9 | 100.00% | No numerical override |\\n| GQS | 7.00 | 5.0 | 100.00% | CASH ≤3: cap 5 |\\n| IAS | NS | NS | 45.00% | R, P, L missing; no reweighting |\\n\\nBQS = 0.20 GOV + 0.20 MOAT + 0.12 REV + 0.10 DUR + 0.12 CAP + 0.10 CASH + 0.08 BAL + 0.08 EXEC = **5.88**, displayed 5.9. GQS = 0.40 REV + 0.20 DUR + 0.15 EARN + 0.15 CAP + 0.10 CASH = **7.00**, capped at **5.00** because CASH=3 reflects persistent weak realization, not merely one growth quarter. There is no extra order-book or growth bonus.\\n\\nIAS = 0.25×5.88 + 0.35 R + 0.20×3 + 0.10 P + 0.10 L. Known contribution **2.07**, missing weight **0.55**, coverage **45%**, arithmetic bounds **2.62–7.57** on the original 1–10 factor scale. These are uncertainty bounds, **not an estimated IAS, ranking or probability**. No missing factor is replaced with 5 or reweighted away. Exact Decimal values and half-up one-decimal displays are retained in the calculation audit.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Supporting facts and contrary evidence | Evidence IDs |\\n|---|---:|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 5 | Medium | FY21–26 original audit and IFC opinions were unmodified; FY26 auditor change was mandatory rotation, not resignation.; FY26 sanctioned co-guarantees were ₹99.2185 crore and related purchases ₹76.3426 crore; CRISIL records India Ratings non-cooperation in 2021. Contrary: Related loans declined, and no substantiated diversion was found. Loan-term wording conflicts and changed IPO deployment still require explanation. | ar2021, ar2022, ar2023, ar2024, ar2025, ar2026, rating2026 |\\n| MOAT | 6 | Medium | Long execution history, qualifications and Gujarat utility relationships support access to T&D tenders.; Management reported 1,300 km of cable and 4,000 RMUs delivered in FY26, with escalation provisions in contracts. Contrary: Tender competition remains; two customers were 56.10% of sales and Gujarat 93% of June backlog. No demonstrated monopoly pricing. | transcript2026, ar2026, rating2026 |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | FY21–26 economically comparable EPC revenue rose from ₹111.60 crore to ₹1,627.94 crore; the pre-pandemic FY20 comparison is also strong.; FY26 restated-basis growth was 51.85%; H2 FY26 versus H2 FY25 was approximately 30.45%. Contrary: The FY21 trough and FY25 step-up inflate headline CAGR. Input-price pass-through and precise constant-price organic growth are not quantified. | ar2021, prospectus, ar2026, presentation2026, transcript2026 |\\n| DUR | 7 | Medium | March 2026 remaining performance obligations were ₹3,326 crore, recognized over one to five years.; June unexecuted orders of ₹3,164 crore and grid reinforcement demand provide an accessible medium-term tender runway. Contrary: Six customers represent 90% of backlog, concentrated in Gujarat. Pipeline is not won cash; BESS economics and finance remain conditional. | ar2026, rating2026, transcript2026 |\\n| CAP | 7 | Medium | The funded-capital bridge includes receivables, retentions, guarantee deposits and fixed assets, not just PPE.; FY25–26 incremental normalized NOPAT / added capital is approximately 25.36%, above the 12–18% analytical discount range. Contrary: Only two aligned Ind AS years support the strongest calculation. Accrued profit needs collection, book PPE is low, longer-window bases are not comparable and BESS returns are unproven. | ar2026, prospectus |\\n| CASH | 3 | High on FY25–26; Medium across classifications | Comparable FY25–26 CFO totals −₹68.1223 crore against ₹239.8316 crore PAT.; FY24 CFO is negative across presentations; FY26 retention is ₹279.4349 crore and current AR ₹348.7721 crore, while deposits absorbed further cash. Contrary: Some earlier restated years show positive CFO and expansion absorbs capital. Repeated recent negative cash realization and supplier funding make this more than a single transient quarter. | ar2024, ar2025, ar2026, prospectus, rating2026 |\\n| BAL | 5 | Medium | Audited debt is ₹127.2338 crore against equity ₹406.1678 crore; operating EBIT covers reported interest about 18 times.; Only ₹1.3452 crore is unrestricted CCE; ₹79.9632 crore deposits are restricted and guarantee exposures are material. Contrary: CRISIL A-/Positive and moderate trailing utilization suggest flexibility, but rated lines are not unconditional available cash and the rating assumes no large debt-funded capex. | ar2026, rating2026, transcript2026 |\\n| EXEC | 6 | Medium | FY25–26 EPC revenue and management-reported physical deliveries grew substantially.; ₹25.1093 crore of original equipment, solar and hydrogen IPO objects remained unspent at 8 August 2026, with redirection proposed to BESS. Contrary: Delivery in EPC is demonstrated; new owned-asset commissioning and the promised cash collection improvement are not yet demonstrated. | ar2026, transcript2026, presentation2026 |\\n| EARN | 7 | Medium | FY26 operating EBIT is ₹196.0215 crore after excluding other income and JV profit and adding back the identified ₹0.6392 crore exception.; Reported diluted EPS increased from ₹59.75 to ₹79.52; FY26 issued shares remained 18,007,392 after the IPO. Contrary: Deferred-tax credit of ₹6.4847 crore and JV profit of ₹5.6911 crore affect PAT. Input-method estimates, mix and historical other income limit pure recurring-earnings confidence. | ar2026, prospectus |\\n| R | NS | Low | The five-year monthly cash model suppresses returns after any opening, pre-entry, intraperiod or terminal funding breach.; Actual entry cash and committed BESS finance remain unverified; the quoted close is only secondary-corroborated. Contrary: A cash-surviving conditional model is useful, but cannot become a supported central investment-return score. | ar2026, transcript2026, model |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Joint collection, supplier-credit, margin and guarantee stress creates finite funding shortfalls.; Owned BESS, restricted margin deposits and connected guarantees can force adverse recapitalization before reported earnings collapse. Contrary: Moderate reported leverage, audited assets and established utility customers offer recovery support. Stress failure does not prove current default or zero recovery. | ar2026, rating2026, model |\\n| P | NS | Low | Reverse growth tests preserve every failed candidate and distinguish funding boundaries from valid return roots.; Price-implied growth depends on future collections, capital intensity and project funding that are not verified at entry. Contrary: Headline P/E appears modest, but cannot on its own resolve retained cash, new project capital or terminal funding requirements. | model, ar2026, market_secondary |\\n| L | NS | Low | The official 19 October security master gives 100-share lots and 18,007,392 shares.; Official BSE 20/60-session trading data, spreads and current restrictions were not retrieved; one secondary 28,200-share print is insufficient. Contrary: Trading is quoted and no suspension is established. Missing BSE rows or NSE absence must not be interpreted as zero volume. | security, market_secondary |\\n\\nComposite sensitivity, with all missing factors preserved and overrides reapplied:\\n\\n| Changed factor | Score shift | BQS | GQS | IAS |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| GOV | -1 | 5.7 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| GOV | 1 | 6.1 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| MOAT | -1 | 5.7 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| MOAT | 1 | 6.1 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CAP | -1 | 5.8 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CAP | 1 | 6.0 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CASH | -1 | 5.8 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CASH | 1 | 6.0 | 7.1 | NS |\\n\\nA score change alone cannot close the funding or trading gates. In particular, a CASH increase to 4 would require new evidence that persistent poor realization has improved; it is not a discretionary way to remove the cap.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nRajesh executes underground cable systems, substations, distribution infrastructure and associated testing, commissioning and maintenance. It traces operating history to 1971, with the present company incorporated in 2010. FY26 operating revenue was ₹1,627.94 crore, overwhelmingly EPC; ₹1,627.47 crore was EPC revenue and ₹0.47 crore other operating revenue. Own solar generation is not the basis of the growth thesis. EPC revenue follows progress/input-cost accounting, while cash follows billing certification, customer payments, advance recovery and retention release. Recognition is therefore not equivalent to collection. Discounts/rebates of ₹21.58 crore are already deducted from contract revenue; do not add them back as normal sales. [FY26 annual report, revenue policy and notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf).\\n\\nThe same industry label conceals three economic models:\\n\\n1. **EPC:** qualify, win, procure, execute, certify, bill, collect. Supplier credit finances part of the work; guarantees and retentions consume capital.\\n2. **Joint ventures:** equity-accounted profit is not parent cash. HKRP and RPSL-VITS carrying value is separated from EPC valuation; no double credit for JV earnings plus its full asset value.\\n3. **Owned BESS:** pay to build and maintain a storage asset, then earn finite capacity charges subject to availability and performance. No fictional EPC sale or internal construction margin is booked on the company's own asset in this model.\\n\\nTwo clients represented 56.10% of FY26 sales. CRISIL reported 93% Gujarat / 7% Odisha June backlog and 90% of backlog with six customers. The utility customer base may reduce some commercial-credit uncertainty but does not remove certification delays, retention conditions, concentration or public-sector procurement risk. [July rating](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html).\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\n**MOAT 6:** execution qualifications, experienced crews, local delivery knowledge and relationships create bidding access, not monopoly pricing. Management described roughly 1,300 km of cable, 4,000 RMUs, 1,200 transformers and 350 feeder panels in FY26; these are management-reported delivery measures rather than independently certified physical audits. Escalation clauses help mitigate input-price exposure but can also inflate reported revenue without equivalent real-volume growth. Geographic expansion must reproduce site execution and collection, not just win tenders. [April earnings call](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Transcription-H2FY26-Earnings-Call-on-23.04.2026.pdf).\\n\\n| Comparator | Relevant economic comparison | Limitation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| KEC International | Global T&D plus other infrastructure; FY25 consolidated revenue ₹21,847 crore, EBITDA margin 7.0%, and NWC 122 days in its dated earnings release. Demonstrates that scale does not eliminate working-capital pressure. | Far larger, diversified and internationally exposed. FY25 EBITDA included ₹24 crore arbitration receipt; no automatic peer multiple applied. |\\n| Transrail Lighting | T&D EPC with tower/conductor manufacturing, substations and other infrastructure. A relevant capability and vertical-integration benchmark. | Different mix and geography; no unverified contemporaneous valuation comparison. |\\n| Kalpataru Projects International | Diversified project execution is a relevant competitive benchmark, not the similarly named real-estate company. | Current comparable financial/multiple dataset not completed; no numerical peer premium inferred. |\\n\\nSources: [KEC FY25 primary earnings release](https://www.kecrpg.com/press-release/earnings/financial-performance-for-quarter-%26-year-ended-31-march-2025-26-05-25), [Transrail primary business and annual-report index](https://transrail.in/investors/financial-reports/annual-reports/), [KPIL primary investor index](https://www.kalpataruprojects.com/investors/financials/annual-reports). These are business comparisons, not a claim that peer fair values validate Rajesh's price.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n| Years | Period | Starting revenue ₹cr | Ending revenue ₹cr | CAGR |\\n|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 3 | 2023–2026 | 207.18 | 1,627.94 | 98.81% |\\n| 5 | 2021–2026 | 111.60 | 1,627.94 | 70.92% |\\n| 6 | 2020–2026 | 167.72 | 1,627.94 | 46.05% |\\n\\n| Fiscal year | Revenue addition ₹cr | Growth |\\n|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 2021 | -56.12 | -33.46% |\\n| 2022 | 35.21 | 31.55% |\\n| 2023 | 60.37 | 41.12% |\\n| 2024 | 77.79 | 37.55% |\\n| 2025 | 787.10 | 276.20% |\\n| 2026 | 555.87 | 51.85% |\\n\\nThe FY21 pandemic trough exaggerates five-year CAGR, but the FY20–26 pre-pandemic cross-check is also strong. FY26 growth over the **restated** FY25 base was 51.85%; H2 FY26 revenue ₹990.12 crore versus ₹759.01 crore was approximately 30.45% higher. The evidence supports genuine expansion in project activity, not a pure price or accounting story. It does **not** quantify the exact split between cable/input inflation, mix, work-volume and pricing. No acquisition contribution is assumed to explain the EPC surge; a precise constant-price organic CAGR remains ND. [Presentation](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Investor-Presentation-RPSL-H2FY26.pdf), [prospectus](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Prospectus_RPSL.pdf).\\n\\n**Comparability control:** original FY25 consolidated revenue ₹1,107.44 crore / PAT ₹93.37 crore changed to ₹1,072.07 crore / ₹96.63 crore under Ind AS, primarily with equity accounting of the JV. FY26 comparisons use the restated figures. Older standalone EPC revenue is economically cross-checked against the later scope, not falsely labelled a fully restated six-year Ind AS series. The revenue schedule starts at 9 for CAGR≥30%; the assigned **8** reflects Medium confidence and basis/driver limits without multiplying arbitrary penalties for the same pandemic base.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nMarch 2026 remaining performance obligations were ₹3,326 crore, with recognition over **one to five years**, not all within 18 months. June unexecuted orders were ₹3,164 crore in CRISIL's July report. These are dated snapshots, not additive amounts. Secondary October announcements of ₹471.92 crore and ₹69.004 crore include taxes; they are not mechanically added to the older backlog without execution, cancellation and GST reconciliation. [FY26 annual report](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf), [rating](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html).\\n\\nA bottom-up growth path requires qualified labour/subcontractors, procurement capacity, utility work fronts, incremental receivables and retention funding, BG lines and cash margin. FY25–26 funded operating capital rose ₹216.44 crore while normalized NOPAT rose ₹54.89 crore, an accrued incremental proxy of 25.36%. This is promising, but collected cash and new-asset returns are different tests. The base forecast explicitly finances rising working capital and collateral from operations rather than converting backlog directly to free cash flow.\\n\\n### BESS: finite asset, not a free growth option\\n\\nThe 65 MW / 130 MWh project at ₹1.89 lakh/MW/month produces **₹14.742 crore annual gross capacity charges**, ₹176.904 crore over twelve years before costs. Management's ₹1.5–2.5 crore/MW cost range implies ₹97.5–162.5 crore project capital. The ₹25.1093 crore redirected IPO allocation is only one equity/internal contribution, not total project cost. Discussions for 70–80% bank finance are not a sanction. Management's indicated 10–12% IRR is a statement, not an independently verified return. [Issuer BESS update](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/20260306_%20Press%20Release%20for%20BESS%20update_.pdf), [earnings call](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Transcription-H2FY26-Earnings-Call-on-23.04.2026.pdf).\\n\\nEven with **zero** operating cost, tax and replacement, twelve-year monthly tariff PV at commissioning is only ₹96.24 crore at 12% and ₹85.27 crore at 15%, below the low ₹97.5 crore gross capex case. This is a useful upper-bound test, **not** proof the subsidized project cannot work. The Phase VII RfS provides potential VGF of ₹18 lakh/MWh, or **₹23.4 crore**, released 20% at financial closure, 50% at COD and 30% one year later, subject to certification and government receipts. A separate BG equal to the entire grant is needed before the first tranche. At an assumed 25% margin, ₹5.85 crore is tied up before a ₹4.68 crore first payment; 100% collateral and payment delay are also stressed. Issuer-specific sanction and unchanged signed BESPA terms remain ND. [Original GUVNL RfS, mirrored document, sections 8–12 and 19–23](https://solarquarter.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/1753286163254.pdf).\\n\\nUsing the disclosed 6 March 2026 BESPA date, the original RfS implies financial closure by 6 December 2026 and COD by 6 September 2027, subject to any signed amendments. The conditional model assumes closure on 19 November 2026, not an uncharged late closure. September 19 is a reporting-bucket end, **not** a shifted COD date. Actual-day overlap allocates each of the 144 contractual monthly periods into reporting buckets; tariff, depreciation and operating costs share that same schedule.\\n\\nThe model's ordinary base recognizes **zero unverified grant receipts**. Separate conditional branches recognize the grant with three- and twelve-month delays, collateral and lower depreciable project basis. Availability, round-trip efficiency and augmentation can further reduce cash. The RfS calls for 95% availability, 85% round-trip efficiency, performance deductions and developer-funded augmentation; a ₹30 crore year-eight replacement is an analytical stress, not a vendor quote. The twelve-month delay bear assumes termination rather than silently keeping full tariff cash after the RfS's nine-month delay window. Existing PBG exposure is not added twice to normal BG outstanding.\\n\\n**GQS 5.0 capped:** the runway is substantial, but repeated negative cash realization and uncertain asset financing prevent a high growth-quality conclusion.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nPromoters controlled 72.70% at March 2026. The IPO combined ₹93.465 crore fresh issuance with ₹67 crore OFS: OFS belongs to selling shareholders, not the company. Authorized capital permits 25 million shares, but authorization is not permission, demand or cash for a future issue. The model's rescue is deliberately hypothetical and bounded.\\n\\n| Commitment / observation | Dated evidence | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Original IPO equipment, solar and hydrogen projects | ₹25.1093 crore remained unspent as of 8 August 2026; proposed redirection to BESS | Allocation slippage and changed risk mix, not evidence of cash theft |\\n| FY26 EPC delivery | Revenue and management-reported physical execution expanded | Positive execution evidence; collection still lags |\\n| BESS commissioning | Management indicated September 2027 | Monitor actual financial closure, equipment ordering, commissioning and grant certification |\\n| Bank funding | Management discussed 70–80% project debt | Not committed; no lender term sheet or full project funding accepted as available |\\n| New Zealand subsidiary | Incorporation confirmed 14 August; filing dated 15 August 2026 | New geographic option, not awarded revenue or proven overseas economics |\\n| Auditor change | FY26 mandatory rotation | Not characterized as a suspicious resignation |\\n\\nFY21–26 full original main audit opinions, CARO where applicable and IFC annexures were reviewed, not merely the prospectus restatement certificate. Opinions were unmodified; latest audit trail operation and preservation were reported. Revenue recognized using costs/progress was a FY26 key audit matter. Reliance on other subsidiary/JV auditors is disclosed, not concealed. Audit cleanliness does not establish receivable collectability or the quality of future capex. [Annual reports in section 15].\\n\\nGovernance watch items include ₹99.2185 crore **sanctioned** co-guarantees for sister concerns, ₹76.3426 crore related-party purchases, four executive directors’ remuneration of ₹8.21 crore (excluding additional related-person salaries), and loans declining from ₹13.1078 crore to ₹6.2417 crore. Sanctioned guarantee face value is neither automatically debt outstanding nor a current cash payment. FY22–24 CARO wording says both that repayment schedules are stipulated and that all loans are on demand/without specified terms; the contradiction is retained. Earlier footer CIN inconsistencies and preferential-allotment boilerplate were checked against current identity/share capital rather than treated as a different issuer or fabricated dilution. CRISIL also preserves a 2021 India Ratings non-cooperation history for missing monitoring information. No substantiated diversion or severe governance breach was established.\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\nAll monetary amounts below are ₹ crore. Earlier rows have their own basis; do not sum them as a clean, comparable cash series.\\n\\n| FY | Basis | Revenue | Operating EBITDA | PAT | CFO | CFO less cash capex | Debt | Equity |\\n|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 2020 | Original standalone IGAAP comparative only | 167.72 | 7.00 | 2.49 | 12.31 | 10.90 | 28.55 | 46.75 |\\n| 2021 | Original standalone IGAAP; original CFO classification | 111.60 | 10.60 | 4.37 | 15.88 | 15.36 | 48.32 | 49.22 |\\n| 2022 | IPO restated standalone IGAAP | 146.81 | 11.03 | 3.45 | 6.52 | 5.03 | 62.43 | 52.29 |\\n| 2023 | IPO restated standalone IGAAP | 207.18 | 14.01 | 6.75 | 15.36 | 11.77 | 59.71 | 58.66 |\\n| 2024 | IPO restated standalone IGAAP | 284.97 | 33.96 | 26.02 | -14.66 | -16.18 | 77.67 | 84.30 |\\n| 2025 | FY26 Ind AS restated consolidated; JV equity method | 1,072.07 | 123.89 | 96.63 | -26.70 | -28.37 | 77.86 | 264.88 |\\n| 2026 | Audited consolidated Ind AS; JV equity method | 1,627.94 | 197.16 | 143.20 | -41.42 | -43.60 | 127.23 | 406.17 |\\n\\n### Earnings to cash\\n\\nFY26 reported PBT ₹195.6423 crore plus finance ₹10.9013 crore, less other income ₹5.4702 crore and JV profit ₹5.6911 crore, plus identified labour-code exception ₹0.6392 crore, gives normalized **operating EBIT ₹196.0215 crore**, EBITDA ₹197.1591 crore. A 25% normalized tax assumption gives NOPAT ₹147.0161 crore. It is not a forecast of actual tax. Reported PAT includes a ₹6.4847 crore deferred-tax credit and ₹4.0834 crore prior-year tax charge. The JV CFS adjustment ₹5.6973 crore differs from P&L ₹5.6911 crore by ₹0.0062 crore; unresolved but immaterial to the conclusion.\\n\\nCash generation did not follow profit. FY26 receivables absorbed ₹171.1229 crore, other financial assets ₹161.0640 crore and inventory ₹62.0980 crore; trade payables supplied ₹207.8024 crore. CFO was **−₹41.4185 crore**, even before ₹2.1837 crore cash fixed/intangible capex and ₹26.4862 crore investing cash deposited with banks. FY25–26 CFO/PAT was **-28.40%**. “No cash losses” in CARO refers to accounting cash-profit tests, not positive operating cash flow.\\n\\nFY26 unrestricted CCE is **₹1.3452 crore**. Current bank margin deposits ₹64.1066 crore plus noncurrent ₹15.8566 crore total **₹79.9632 crore** and are retained as restricted operating capital. ₹348.7721 crore current receivables and ₹279.4349 crore retention together equal about 141 days of FY26 sales on closing balances. Retention release can follow six-month to two-year defect-liability periods. These cannot be treated as cash equivalents. Inventory ₹124.9462 crore is financed partly by ₹328.3156 crore payables and ₹25.0565 crore customer advances. Inventory/payable days and a conventional CCC are ND rather than calculated with an incompatible sales denominator.\\n\\n### Funded-capital reconciliation and incremental return\\n\\nOperating IC = equity ₹406.1678 + debt ₹127.2338 − free cash ₹1.3452 − JV ₹23.1474 − all loans ₹6.7871 − deferred-tax asset ₹8.8713 + current tax liability ₹10.1630 = **₹503.4136 crore**. Asset-side bridge: narrow ONWC ₹399.7811 + residual operating-net items ₹4.4084 + restricted deposits ₹79.9632 + fixed/CWIP ₹19.2609 = the same total. The residual is a financing reconciliation and includes a tiny unpaid-dividend classification; it is not a fully itemized “pure” trade-working-capital account. Unverified recoveries and restricted deposit release are not added to cash.\\n\\nFY25 IC ₹286.9773 crore and opening FY24 Ind AS IC ₹141.6979 crore anchor the comparison. FY26 NOPAT / average FY25–26 IC = **37.20%**. Incremental FY25–26 NOPAT / incremental IC = **25.36%**; the two-year mixed-basis proxy = **33.75%**. **Three- and five-year incremental-capital windows are ND** because older cash/deposit classification and consolidation bases are not sufficiently reconciled; the one- and two-year proxies are not relabelled as substitutes. These are accrual return proxies, not project IRRs or proof new capex itself earned the entire profit uplift. Margin/mix gains on old operations, low book PPE, supplier funding and uncollected retention all matter. The CAP score therefore remains 7, not 9–10.\\n\\n### Debt, guarantees and accounting vintages\\n\\nDebt ₹127.2338 crore comprises current ₹81.5882 crore and noncurrent ₹45.6456 crore. Related-party debt ₹45.0023 crore is not converted to equity in the model; CRISIL's treatment of 75% of ₹43.34 crore promoter loans as quasi-equity is a rating adjustment, not legal extinguishment. Agreements defer related-loan repayment until after 31 March 2028. Scenario repayment dates for facilities are explicit assumptions, not invented maturities. Bank guarantees are ₹312.7171 crore. CRISIL's ₹151 crore CC / ₹212 crore BG ratings and 34.04% trailing average utilization do not establish drawable October headroom. Its rationale assumes absence of large/debt-funded capex, which must be reconciled with BESS rather than treated as its underwriting.\\n\\nOriginal versus later CFO presentations are preserved: FY21 original ₹15.8778 crore versus FY22 comparative ₹13.9185; FY22 original **−₹10.8969** versus IPO-restated **+₹6.5191**; FY23 original ₹17.2187 versus restated ₹15.3579; FY24 original **−₹25.3843**, IPO-restated **−₹14.6624** and FY25 comparative **−₹22.1081**. The FY24 restatement moves approximately ₹10.7217 crore to noncurrent-asset investing outflow. No cumulative five-year CFO ratio is presented as comparable.\\n\\nCash-flow net IPO proceeds ₹85.3825 crore deduct ₹8.0825 crore issue expenses, whereas objects net ₹77.9693 crore deduct ₹15.4957 crore overall costs. Allocation/timing needs reconciliation. The CARO statement that ₹25.1093 crore unspent IPO proceeds were parked in the **cash-credit account** means the amount is not independently added as a second cash asset. Current CCE and drawn CC already reflect the balance-sheet position. The FY25 weighted average EPS denominator is **16,170,642**; FY26 actual/weighted shares are 18,007,392. Using ₹59.75→₹79.52 reported EPS avoids dividing both periods by the wrong denominator.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\nAt ₹821.85, market cap is ₹1,479.94 crore, reported FY26 P/E **10.33×**, normalized operating earnings P/E **10.66×**, book P/B **3.64×**. Historical EV uses debt less only free CCE, not restricted deposits. After separately removing JV and nonoperating loan carrying values, illustrative EPC EV/EBITDA is **8.00×**, EV/EBIT **8.04×**. These are March-balance-sheet multiples at the October price, not verified October net-debt multiples. The reported earnings yield is not a distributable cash yield.\\n\\n### Linked cash model\\n\\nThe model starts at audited 31 March 2026 cash and carries seven monthly pre-entry periods to 19 October, then 60 monthly periods. Opening cash is tested before any receipts. Each period shows early operating receipts, working capital, collateral, EPC maintenance/growth capex, BESS, tax, interest, maturities and late receipts. There is no opening-cash reset at entry. The base reaches **₹59.80 crore modeled entry cash**, but this depends on collection/retention normalization and is **not observed October cash**.\\n\\n| Input | Bear | Base | Bull |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Pre-entry annualized sales / EBITDA margin | ₹1,450cr / 7.5% | ₹1,800cr / 11.5% | ₹2,000cr / 12.5% |\\n| Five forecast annual sales ₹cr | 1,450; 1,370; 1,440; 1,550; 1,650 | 2,000; 2,280; 2,550; 2,800; 3,000 | 2,200; 2,700; 3,250; 3,800; 4,300 |\\n| EBITDA margins | 7.5%; 7%; 7.5%; 8%; 8% | 11% throughout | 12.5%; 12.5%; 12%; 12%; 12% |\\n| AR / retention / inventory, % sales | 27 / 22 / 9 | 19 / 16 / 7.5 | 17 / 14 / 7 |\\n| Payables / advances, % sales | 16 / 1.2 | 20 / 1.5 | 21 / 1.7 |\\n| Restricted deposits, % sales floor | 5.5 | 5.0 | 4.5 |\\n| BESS total / operating cost / annual maintenance ₹cr | 162.5 / 4.0 / 1.5 | 130 / 3.0 / 1.0 | 97.5 / 2.5 / 0.8 |\\n| BESS delay | 12 months, termination stress | No delay | No delay |\\n\\nAll these are A. Receipts are 70% before requirements and 30% after, stressed to 30%/100%. Cash floor ₹1 crore is an assumption tested at ₹5/10 crore; it is not a bank covenant. No restricted collateral is automatically released on slowing sales. Historical tax payable, pre-entry dividend, loan interest, debt run-off, default guarantee stress and whole-share dilution all flow through cash.\\n\\n| Path | Cash survives? | Minimum cash ₹cr | Year-five cash ₹cr | Earned EPC terminal EV ₹cr | Finite BESS terminal EV ₹cr | Five-year scenario IRR |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| bear_none | No | -400.70 | -224.31 | 660.90 | 0.00 | NS |\\n| bear_bounded_optional | No | -400.70 | -224.31 | 660.90 | 0.00 | NS |\\n| base_none | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 562.00 | 2,065.14 | 32.59 | 12.67% |\\n| base_bounded_optional | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 562.00 | 2,065.14 | 32.59 | 12.67% |\\n| bull_none | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 956.92 | 3,361.54 | 32.10 | 24.23% |\\n| bull_bounded_optional | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 956.92 | 3,361.54 | 32.10 | 24.23% |\\n\\nA cash breach is sticky. Negative balances may continue in the diagnostic ledger to expose the size of the financing gap, but they do not become ordinary returns or an executable recovery value. Optional bank/equity paths never repair a pre-entry breach. The zero-new-finance base and bull assume sufficient internally realized cash; that result is conditional, not evidence that a lender has committed funds.\\n\\nBase annual cash bridge:\\n\\n| Forecast year | Sales | EPC EBITDA | ΔONWC | Deposits added | EPC capex | BESS capex | Tax paid | Interest | Debt repaid | Closing cash |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 1 | 2,000.00 | 220.00 | 42.60 | 10.00 | 4.14 | 115.73 | 51.56 | 12.72 | 0.00 | 47.15 |\\n| 2 | 2,280.00 | 250.80 | 59.64 | 14.00 | 5.48 | 0.00 | 59.43 | 12.72 | 127.23 | 33.39 |\\n| 3 | 2,550.00 | 280.50 | 57.51 | 13.50 | 5.49 | 0.00 | 69.99 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 181.74 |\\n| 4 | 2,800.00 | 308.00 | 53.25 | 12.50 | 5.35 | 0.00 | 76.83 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 356.55 |\\n| 5 | 3,000.00 | 330.00 | 42.60 | 10.00 | 4.74 | 0.00 | 82.29 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 562.00 |\\n\\n### Earned terminal value, not an assumed exit multiple\\n\\nEPC terminal FCFF uses actual modeled capital, maintenance equal to modeled depreciation, full incremental receivables/retentions/inventory less operating liabilities, restricted deposits and fixed investment. At base 4% growth / 15% discount, next-year NOPAT is **₹256.03 crore**, operating capital **₹834.00 crore**, incremental growth investment **₹33.36 crore**, and FCFF **₹227.17 crore**. Implied NOPAT / terminal capital is **30.70%**, reported as an earned model result rather than a freely chosen target ROIC. EPC FCFF/(discount−growth) is primary; no arbitrary P/E or EV/EBITDA rerating creates the return.\\n\\nBESS receives only the remaining twelve-year contract cash flows, with maintenance and augmentation; no perpetual terminal tariff, salvage or automatic renewal. JV is valued once at ₹23.1474 crore carrying value; nonoperating loans receive 50%/80%/100% recovery assumptions. No JV profit enters EPC EBITDA or cash flow. A further twelve-month continuation retains all monthly operating, BESS, debt and capital requirements; terminal reserve equals the maximum cumulative funding gap plus the cash floor. Only surplus cash above that reserve enters equity. Base reserve is **₹1.00 crore**. No operating working capital or BG deposits are also liquidated into terminal equity.\\n\\n| Discount rate | Terminal EPC growth | Conditional terminal price ₹ | Scenario IRR | Entry unlevered DCF cross-check ₹/share |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 12.00% | 2.00% | 1,673.49 | 15.28% | 980.11 |\\n| 12.00% | 4.00% | 1,923.66 | 18.54% | 1,122.06 |\\n| 12.00% | 6.00% | 2,340.61 | 23.28% | 1,358.65 |\\n| 15.00% | 2.00% | 1,365.82 | 10.69% | 711.12 |\\n| 15.00% | 4.00% | 1,492.09 | 12.67% | 773.91 |\\n| 15.00% | 6.00% | 1,674.49 | 15.30% | 864.59 |\\n| 18.00% | 2.00% | 1,173.19 | 7.38% | 545.67 |\\n| 18.00% | 4.00% | 1,245.07 | 8.66% | 577.09 |\\n| 18.00% | 6.00% | 1,340.91 | 10.29% | 618.98 |\\n\\nThe terminal ledger separately reports whether forward-year net cash is nonnegative after all requirements; inherited cash sufficient for one year does not establish perpetual self-funding. The 12/15/18% discount grid is an analytical nominal-INR risk sensitivity, **not** a precisely estimated WACC and not automatically equal to the equity hurdle. The DCF cross-check discounts actual modeled five-year unlevered FCFF and earned terminal operating value, then adjusts entry cash/debt and separately valued assets. For optional financing it is not old-shareholder value without explicit financier-claim valuation. Conditional terminal wealth includes dilution, fees and remaining debt. Terminal value dependence, collection improvement and BESS cost are material model risks.\\n\\nConditional base terminal value supports these five-year hurdle-entry arithmetic levels, **not buy prices** while G3/G4 remain unresolved:\\n\\n| Equity hurdle | Conditional entry ₹ |\\n|---:|---:|\\n| 12.00% | 846.65 |\\n| 15.00% | 741.83 |\\n| 18.00% | 652.21 |\\n\\nReverse tests solve annual sales CAGR from a ₹1,800 crore starting run-rate while fixing base margin/capital ratios. Each tested candidate recomputes the full cash path. The search domain is 0–60% CAGR; failed funding candidates are retained, and a financing boundary is never called a return root.\\n\\n| Target | Five-year wealth multiple | Retained tests | Cash-surviving growth root / result |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| 0.12 | 1.76 | 39 | 10.10%–10.10% |\\n| 0.15 | 2.01 | 39 | 14.22%–14.22% |\\n| 0.18 | 2.29 | 13 | No cash-surviving sign-change found |\\n| 5x | 5.00 | 13 | No cash-surviving sign-change found |\\n| 10x | 10.00 | 13 | No cash-surviving sign-change found |\\n\\nSecondary multiple-normalization diagnostics keep the identical cash path, restricted-capital reserve, finite BESS value, financing and dilution. The unchanged historical EPC multiple is 8.00×; 6× normalization and 4× compression do not require rerating. These do not replace the primary earned-FCFF valuation.\\n\\n| Path | Multiple case | EPC EV/EBITDA | Diagnostic terminal price ₹ | Conditional IRR |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| bear_none | compression | 4.00 | 307.80 | NS |\\n| bear_none | normalization | 6.00 | 454.41 | NS |\\n| bear_none | unchanged_historical_EPC_multiple | 8.00 | 600.70 | NS |\\n| base_none | compression | 4.00 | 1,078.30 | 5.58% |\\n| base_none | normalization | 6.00 | 1,444.81 | 11.94% |\\n| base_none | unchanged_historical_EPC_multiple | 8.00 | 1,810.55 | 17.11% |\\n| bull_none | compression | 4.00 | 1,711.19 | 15.80% |\\n| bull_none | normalization | 6.00 | 2,284.29 | 22.69% |\\n| bull_none | unchanged_historical_EPC_multiple | 8.00 | 2,856.18 | 28.29% |\\n\\nAt the primary base discount/growth, five-year operating FCFF PV is **₹389.95 crore**, terminal operating value PV is **₹1,042.94 crore**, and terminal value supplies **72.79%** of the operating enterprise DCF. Nonoperating assets and entry cash/debt are outside that denominator. Dependence can exceed 100% in cash-consuming cases; it is not a probability or assurance.\\n\\nExecution costs, spread and market impact cannot be precisely verified without the BSE tape and order book. The following analytical sensitivities charge both entry and exit cash flows, retain zero interim dividends, and are before investor tax; they are not claims of executable transaction costs.\\n\\n| Entry cost | Exit cost | Entry paid ₹/share | Terminal net ₹/share | Base IRR after costs |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 0.00% | 0.00% | 821.85 | 1,492.09 | 12.67% |\\n| 0.50% | 0.50% | 825.96 | 1,484.63 | 12.44% |\\n| 1.00% | 1.00% | 830.07 | 1,477.17 | 12.22% |\\n| 2.00% | 2.00% | 838.29 | 1,462.25 | 11.77% |\\n\\nThe archive also retains the full 3×3 margin/collection grid, 3×3 discount/growth grid, ₹5/10 crore cash-floor tests, receipt timing, ₹97.5/162.5 crore BESS capex, CWIP allocation, guarantee calls, VGF collateral/delay and BESS-delay tests. There are **91 unique cash paths and 149 test references**; exact duplicates share a complete path rather than disappear. All failed paths, brackets and terminal continuations are preserved.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is not “orders stop growing.” It is **recognized growth keeps consuming cash while customer collections slow and supplier finance tightens**, at the same time that BESS requires equity, bank guarantees and capex. Margin compression can reduce cash available just as operating capital expands. A guarantee call or related-party exposure could force financing before the accounting loss becomes visible.\\n\\nThe linked bear uses lower sales/margins, 27% AR plus 22% retention, reduced supplier support, 5% receivable/retention loss funding, 10% of other bank guarantees (excluding the separately called ₹8.125 crore BESS PBG) and 20% co-guarantee cash calls, BESS overrun/termination, and debt run-off. The early cash breach cannot be repaired by hypothetical equity arriving after entry. Asset recovery and creditor priority are uncertain; no unsupported “zero recovery” or neat negative IRR is supplied.\\n\\nSeparate base-path guarantee stresses test the rescue mechanism. New debt is capped at ₹100 crore gross, charges 11% including a full issue-month interest reserve, and amortizes without assumed refinancing. Equity is capped at ₹100 crore gross at ₹657.48/share, with 3% fees, integer shares and authorized-share headroom. Neither is committed. The severe ₹411.9356 crore call is a full-face exposure stress, **not** an assertion that all guarantees will be called or all co-guaranteed debt is drawn.\\n\\n| Stress / financing | Survives? | Optional bank ₹cr | Optional equity gross ₹cr | Final shares | Dilution |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| guarantee_shock_150_none | No; returns NS | 0.00 | 0.00 | 18007392 | 0.00% |\\n| guarantee_shock_150_bounded_optional | Yes, conditional | 100.00 | 37.45 | 18577044 | 3.07% |\\n| guarantee_shock_411.9356_none | No; returns NS | 0.00 | 0.00 | 18007392 | 0.00% |\\n| guarantee_shock_411.9356_bounded_optional | No; returns NS | 100.00 | 100.00 | 19528350 | 7.79% |\\n\\nThesis-break variable: **cash tied up per additional rupee of executed revenue**, including retentions and collateral, relative to credible cash earnings and drawable finance. Recovery of old receivables without replacement by larger new retentions is more decisive than an order announcement.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nFivefold wealth in five years requires approximately **37.97% CAGR**; tenfold requires **58.49%** before investor tax. They are outcomes to test, not forecasts. At unchanged shares and no dividends, fivefold price means ₹4,109.25 and tenfold ₹8,218.50, with equity values roughly ₹7,400 crore and ₹14,799 crore before dilution. The current audited scale does not make either automatic.\\n\\nThe cash-linked fixed-margin reverse tests above found no cash-surviving sign change for 5× or 10× within the tested 0–60% revenue-CAGR range. That is **not a mathematical proof of impossibility outside the tested assumptions**: margins, capital efficiency, grants, new equity or business mix could change. But funded scaling, per-share dilution and terminal reinvestment must change with them. BESS's ₹14.742 crore annual gross tariff cannot by itself justify a multi-thousand-crore equity rerating. A possible exceptional outcome is not a scoreable base thesis.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n| KPI / baseline | Evidence to obtain | Warning / thesis-break threshold |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Free cash ₹1.3452cr at March 2026 | September cash flow, bank balance classification and actual October bridge | No verified drawable cash to meet essential payments; do not infer from bank deposits |\\n| FY25–26 CFO/PAT negative | Half-year and full-year collections, retention releases and aged balances | CFO remains negative despite slower growth, or operating capital rises faster than accrued profit |\\n| AR + retention ₹628.2070cr | Customer/project ageing, certification and defect-liability dates | Material overdue/uncertified balances, disputed claims or retention release slippage |\\n| Restricted deposits ₹79.9632cr | BG margin terms, expiry, utilization and release permissions | Collateral demand outpaces available cash; no automatic margin release assumed |\\n| Debt ₹127.2338cr | Sanctions, covenants, renewal, repayment schedule and undrawn availability | Renewal denied or callable debt cannot be serviced without unsupported rescue |\\n| BESS total cost / finance ND | Signed EPC/battery contract, funding tie-up and grant sanction | Cost above ₹162.5cr or closure delayed without funded contingency; contract delay/termination exposure |\\n| VGF potential ₹23.4cr | CEA certification, government receipt and BG cash-margin terms | First receipt delayed while full BG cash margin is locked; no grant double count |\\n| BESS availability / augmentation | Signed performance schedule, warranties, replacement budget | Availability/efficiency deductions or augmentation exceed model cash margins |\\n| Related guarantees ₹99.2185cr sanctioned | Actual debt drawn, beneficiary health and releases | Connected exposure rises faster than capital or is called without reimbursement |\\n| BSE lot 100; 60-session tape ND | Official daily volume/value, no-trade days, spreads, circuit/restriction notices | Reference exit exceeds acceptable participation capacity or effectively cannot execute |\\n\\nA potential catalyst is evidence, not merely an announcement: improved cash conversion, verified project closure, on-time commissioning or demonstrated geographic diversification. Failure to obtain evidence keeps the relevant gate unresolved.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Hold — unresolved funding and tradability; pending evidence.** Not Avoid–governance, not a finding of insolvency, and not a low-IAS stock automatically called expensive. BQS 5.9 reflects useful but contestable execution capability. GQS 5.0 reflects the cash override despite strong growth. IAS remains NS because R/P/L cannot be defensibly scored at the reference price with current evidence. The scoreable BQS/GQS arithmetic can be compared descriptively, but this gated record should not be promoted into investable rankings.\\n\\nRajesh does not meet compounder thresholds of BQS≥8 and GQS≥7, irrespective of any future price gain. A lower price can improve modeled returns, but cannot establish free cash, a lender commitment, subsidy receipt or market liquidity. Reassess only after the specific funding and trading gaps are closed and the actual post-March cash bridge replaces assumptions. Nothing here authorizes or recommends a trade or a portfolio allocation.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n**Evidence legend:** V = verified public historical fact; M = management statement; A = analyst assumption/output; ND = not disclosed or verified; NS = not scoreable. Original issuer PDFs, OCR, fetched web bodies, transfer helpers and private service identifiers are not redistributed. Source URLs, hashes where bytes were obtained, page references, authored factual extracts, model code and full calculation rows are retained. Hashes verify the retrieved edition, not authenticity beyond the source provenance.\\n\\nKey assumptions are deliberately exposed: the March-to-October cash bridge; annual sales and margins; receipt timing; receivable/retention/payable intensity; deposit margins; maintenance and replacement; debt run-off; 25% normalized tax; hypothetical rescue price/caps; VGF certification delay and collateral; 12/15/18% discount; 2/4/6% terminal growth; JV/loan recovery and no post-entry dividends. ₹1 crore plant-and-machinery CWIP is **not explicitly identified as BESS** in the audit; base allocation is A and a zero-BESS-allocation alternative is retained. Fees, source vintage differences and unknown exact maturity schedules are not invented away.\\n\\nOpen questions with the greatest decision impact:\\n\\n1. What are unrestricted cash and genuinely drawable, covenant-compliant facilities at entry, after all BG margins and committed capex?\\n2. How much of the FY26 receivable and retention balance has actually been collected, and how much was replaced by new locked capital?\\n3. What are binding BESS total cost, availability/efficiency deductions, augmentation obligations, warranties and exact contractual commissioning dates?\\n4. Is issuer-specific VGF sanctioned, what are certification and disbursement dates, and how much BG collateral is required? Does the final BESPA amend the original RfS?\\n5. How do IPO expense allocation and cash-flow classifications reconcile, and what is the exact accounting nature of residual funds parked in CC?\\n6. What are current connected-party guarantee utilization, reimbursement security and terms? Can co-guarantees be reduced?\\n7. What is official BSE liquidity over at least 20/60 sessions and the actual spread/restriction state? A 28,200-share secondary daily print does not answer this.\\n\\nSource register:\\n\\n- **ar2021**: [Original audit and CARO/IFC PDF29–41; financials42–44; FY20 comparative](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2020-21.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 020ce8d653f3c3aff6653aba5716702e0cfa768852f05c8bb21b5928e3221473.\\n- **ar2022**: [Original audit/CARO/IFC PDF29–44; financials45–47; notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2021-22.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 fbf36fb3268d9c491bd6ad2a806813298159fa5849f195cecfdd677e3daaad86.\\n- **ar2023**: [Original audit/CARO/IFC PDF31–46; financials47–49; notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2022-23.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 47e4f6d75f7b902be38f6930978039b5993fb3713a39b1140893b6883da391b3.\\n- **ar2024**: [Original audit/CARO/IFC PDF41–57; financials58–60; notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2023-24.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 ba3bf059e96e6d7725e9c276f564616b6a11f7eec79e1a592be7aea5a9f29ac9.\\n- **ar2025**: [Original standalone audit/CARO/IFC printed71–77; CFS80; consolidated109–136](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2024-25-Final.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 c468d7035eed77387c8034bd5de5da847196493f206ec16801f168413a51d71c.\\n- **ar2026**: [Revised FY26 report: printed29 BESS;77–78 objects;118–129 standalone audit/CARO/IFC;192–201 consolidated audit/IFC;202–207 statements;226–245 notes;249–265 RPT/transition](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 3dae50c85009f5369a7f9403c9f09bfd6a3c5e802552c63d20352d23e696d3be.\\n- **prospectus**: [28 November 2024 prospectus; printed55–57 restated standalone statements; risk factors; objects; share capital](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Prospectus_RPSL.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 c3858e57fc7f46647f70197228082fb95983dd84006fd2b0844661bfbd54c3cf.\\n- **results2026**: [23 April 2026 audited results, Ind AS comparatives](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Audited-Standalone-Consolidated-Financial-Results-March-2026.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 18b32699958fe3e52bb6eeb6731dcbce816bebdaab0529397599d518ebdef4f9.\\n- **presentation2026**: [23 April 2026 investor presentation pp8–10/21–24, operational delivery and historical summaries](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Investor-Presentation-RPSL-H2FY26.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 6c89f782d0dc1a2310ccf0f1d77fcaf7e5111c0d2403191303f22447b6e5a30f.\\n- **transcript2026**: [23 April 2026 call pp8–10 collections,17–22 BESS and finance,31–32 returns](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Transcription-H2FY26-Earnings-Call-on-23.04.2026.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 ad4aecdd27d8febde69bfaac7ecbd91af2327b80c675e8b43b3719f2a28f90b7.\\n- **share2026**: [31 March 2026 statutory shareholding pattern, promoter and encumbrance declarations](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Shareholding%20Pattern%20-%2031.03.2026.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 58b24c22f940c58be8c992e90c88599eeabc35240cca1e95d9540a946a33d570.\\n- **bess2026**: [6 March 2026 issuer BESS update](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/20260306_%20Press%20Release%20for%20BESS%20update_.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 ee343b22f987e18096c0f4060d85dea907031152aeeb53b98266781c1018e51a.\\n- **bessaward**: [Issuer BESS award press release](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Press%20Release%20_%20BESS%20_%20GUVNL.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 f551f7985dd6e3f2234dc87fb4c565dab99335d6b31e39d64cafbdc0a3743eeb.\\n- **nzsub**: [New Zealand wholly-owned subsidiary incorporated/confirmed 14 August 2026; filing dated 15 August 2026; nil turnover and no operations at disclosure](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/New%20Foreign%20Subsidairy%20Company.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 1136598cbf81102f359591ce225d8cdb06e969d42fffe8d5f8d6bc065a1ec892.\\n- **rating2026**: [Standalone rationale, liquidity, promoter quasi-equity adjustment, facility annexure](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **market_secondary**: [19 October close821.85, volume28200; corroborated StockAnalysis and Goodreturns](https://www.equitypandit.com/historical-data/544291). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **market_crosscheck**: [19 October close821.85, volume28200; postcutoff rows excluded](https://stockanalysis.com/quote/bom/544291/history/). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **security**: [Issuer-only master row: ISIN, shares18007392, lot100,SM; no inference of NSE traded liquidity](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **agm_secondary**: [All AGM resolutions reportedly approved; primary attachment retrieval failed so not counted as confirmed financing](https://bazaarwatch.com/announcement/141592/rajesh-power-services-ltd-scrutinizer-report-and-e-voting-results-for-annual-general-meeting-held-on). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **orders_secondary**: [October12/14 MGVCL471.92 and UGVCL69.004crore awards including taxes; not added mechanically to March/June backlog](https://www.whalesbook.com/company/announcements/RAJESH). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **bess_rfs**: [Original GUVNL Phase VII RfS mirrored document, 124 pages; sections 8–12, 19, 23: availability, efficiency, augmentation, delay, VGF and bank guarantees](https://solarquarter.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/1753286163254.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **peer_kec**: [Dated FY25 consolidated revenue, margin, NWC and business mix; not a current valuation comparable](https://www.kecrpg.com/press-release/earnings/financial-performance-for-quarter-%26-year-ended-31-march-2025-26-05-25). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **peer_transrail**: [Primary business navigation and annual-report index: T&D EPC and manufacturing comparison](https://transrail.in/investors/financial-reports/annual-reports/). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **peer_kpil**: [Exact corporate issuer primary investor index; no unverified numerical peer multiple](https://www.kalpataruprojects.com/investors/financials/annual-reports). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\nReconstruction: both compact and full packages contain every authored input and standard-library-only deterministic code required to regenerate all outputs. Compact omits bulky generated rows, not reconstruction inputs; full also includes canonical all-path output, valuation rows and the cash-event CSV for immediate inspection. Each package is independently tested after deletion of generated outputs and attestations. Reported archive verification refers to fresh downloaded bytes and regenerated outputs, not only a local cached copy.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"title\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Hold — unresolved funding and tradability. BQS 5.9/10; GQS 5.0/10; IAS NS.** Rajesh Power is a fast-growing electrical transmission and distribution EPC contractor, not a mature utility with recurring cash already in hand. Qualifications, execution scale and Gujarat utility relationships are real strengths. The decisive weakness is conversion: comparable FY25–26 operating cash flow was **negative ₹68.12 crore** against ₹239.83 crore profit. Retentions, receivables and guarantee collateral absorb capital while the proposed owned BESS adds a different, capital-intensive risk. At the secondary-corroborated ₹821.85 close on 19 October 2026, the conditional base model returns 12.67%, below the 15% analytical hurdle. That is a scenario, not a scoreable forecast of current available funding. The bear breaches cash before the investment date, so no ordinary return is shown. This is an **execution-led infrastructure growth thesis with financing risk**, not a proven compounder. Verified collections, drawable facilities, project financing and exchange liquidity could change the conclusion; a low P/E alone cannot. [FY26 annual report](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf), [July rating](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html).\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n| Item | Basis |\\n|---|---|\\n| Legal issuer | Rajesh Power Services Limited; CIN L31300GJ2010PLC059536; ISIN INE0VN801010 |\\n| Listing | BSE SME, code 544291, symbol RAJESH. NSE interoperability master symbol RAJESH$ is **not evidence of an NSE listing**. |\\n| Information cutoff | 20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata. Research retrieved 23 October, using only disclosures available by the cutoff. |\\n| Price | ₹821.85 on 19 October 2026, secondary cross-check, not a verified executable exchange quote |\\n| Shares / market cap | 18,007,392 fully paid shares; market cap ₹1,479.94 crore. Current master lot 100 shares. |\\n| Financial scope | FY26 audited consolidated Ind AS; FY25 restated comparative. Older standalone/IPO-restated series explicitly separated. No H1 FY27 cash results assumed. |\\n| Horizon / hurdle | Five years from 19 October 2026 to 19 October 2031; 15% nominal annualized before investor tax, tested at 12% and 18%. |\\n| Liquidity reference | ₹5 lakh analytical reference, not a personal allocation recommendation. Rounded down to six lots: 600 shares, ₹493,110 before costs. |\\n\\n| Gate | Result | Evidence and consequence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Conditional | Clean audit opinions do not remove connected-party exposure, loan-term inconsistencies or allocation questions. No substantiated severe misconduct; no GOV≤3 override. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Conditional | Latest audited Ind AS statements are usable. Preserve original CFO vintages and transition bridge rather than invent a uniform series. No established material misstatement requiring a Fail. |\\n| G3 Funding / survival | **Hold** | March cash is not October cash. Current drawable terms, renewal, guarantees and BESS financial closure are unverified. Conditional scenario survival is not committed finance. No finding of actual default, so no mechanical IAS≤3 funding cap. |\\n| G4 Tradability | **Hold** | Official BSE 20/60-session tape, spread and restrictions were not obtained. Missing rows are ND, never zero-volume observations. No unsupported suspension claim. |\\n\\nLiquidity capacity remains ND at **2%, 5% and 10% participation** because the conservative denominator, minimum of 60-session mean and median traded value including zero-trade days, is missing. The 20-session mean/median, zero-trade count and spreads are also ND. No exit-days score is fabricated from one print.\\n\\nConfidence: **High** on latest audited P&L, capital and original audit opinions; **Medium** on economically comparable longer-term growth, capital efficiency and order execution; **Low** on actual entry-date unrestricted cash, future BESS funding and executable liquidity. Promoter holding was 72.70%, with no reported pledge in the March 2026 filing; that is dated evidence, not a guarantee of October status. [Shareholding filing](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Shareholding%20Pattern%20-%2031.03.2026.pdf), [official security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz).\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\n| Measure | Exact raw | Displayed final | Evidence coverage | Override / missingness |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| BQS | 5.88 | 5.9 | 100.00% | No numerical override |\\n| GQS | 7.00 | 5.0 | 100.00% | CASH ≤3: cap 5 |\\n| IAS | NS | NS | 45.00% | R, P, L missing; no reweighting |\\n\\nBQS = 0.20 GOV + 0.20 MOAT + 0.12 REV + 0.10 DUR + 0.12 CAP + 0.10 CASH + 0.08 BAL + 0.08 EXEC = **5.88**, displayed 5.9. GQS = 0.40 REV + 0.20 DUR + 0.15 EARN + 0.15 CAP + 0.10 CASH = **7.00**, capped at **5.00** because CASH=3 reflects persistent weak realization, not merely one growth quarter. There is no extra order-book or growth bonus.\\n\\nIAS = 0.25×5.88 + 0.35 R + 0.20×3 + 0.10 P + 0.10 L. Known contribution **2.07**, missing weight **0.55**, coverage **45%**, arithmetic bounds **2.62–7.57** on the original 1–10 factor scale. These are uncertainty bounds, **not an estimated IAS, ranking or probability**. No missing factor is replaced with 5 or reweighted away. Exact Decimal values and half-up one-decimal displays are retained in the calculation audit.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Supporting facts and contrary evidence | Evidence IDs |\\n|---|---:|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 5 | Medium | FY21–26 original audit and IFC opinions were unmodified; FY26 auditor change was mandatory rotation, not resignation.; FY26 sanctioned co-guarantees were ₹99.2185 crore and related purchases ₹76.3426 crore; CRISIL records India Ratings non-cooperation in 2021. Contrary: Related loans declined, and no substantiated diversion was found. Loan-term wording conflicts and changed IPO deployment still require explanation. | ar2021, ar2022, ar2023, ar2024, ar2025, ar2026, rating2026 |\\n| MOAT | 6 | Medium | Long execution history, qualifications and Gujarat utility relationships support access to T&D tenders.; Management reported 1,300 km of cable and 4,000 RMUs delivered in FY26, with escalation provisions in contracts. Contrary: Tender competition remains; two customers were 56.10% of sales and Gujarat 93% of June backlog. No demonstrated monopoly pricing. | transcript2026, ar2026, rating2026 |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | FY21–26 economically comparable EPC revenue rose from ₹111.60 crore to ₹1,627.94 crore; the pre-pandemic FY20 comparison is also strong.; FY26 restated-basis growth was 51.85%; H2 FY26 versus H2 FY25 was approximately 30.45%. Contrary: The FY21 trough and FY25 step-up inflate headline CAGR. Input-price pass-through and precise constant-price organic growth are not quantified. | ar2021, prospectus, ar2026, presentation2026, transcript2026 |\\n| DUR | 7 | Medium | March 2026 remaining performance obligations were ₹3,326 crore, recognized over one to five years.; June unexecuted orders of ₹3,164 crore and grid reinforcement demand provide an accessible medium-term tender runway. Contrary: Six customers represent 90% of backlog, concentrated in Gujarat. Pipeline is not won cash; BESS economics and finance remain conditional. | ar2026, rating2026, transcript2026 |\\n| CAP | 7 | Medium | The funded-capital bridge includes receivables, retentions, guarantee deposits and fixed assets, not just PPE.; FY25–26 incremental normalized NOPAT / added capital is approximately 25.36%, above the 12–18% analytical discount range. Contrary: Only two aligned Ind AS years support the strongest calculation. Accrued profit needs collection, book PPE is low, longer-window bases are not comparable and BESS returns are unproven. | ar2026, prospectus |\\n| CASH | 3 | High on FY25–26; Medium across classifications | Comparable FY25–26 CFO totals −₹68.1223 crore against ₹239.8316 crore PAT.; FY24 CFO is negative across presentations; FY26 retention is ₹279.4349 crore and current AR ₹348.7721 crore, while deposits absorbed further cash. Contrary: Some earlier restated years show positive CFO and expansion absorbs capital. Repeated recent negative cash realization and supplier funding make this more than a single transient quarter. | ar2024, ar2025, ar2026, prospectus, rating2026 |\\n| BAL | 5 | Medium | Audited debt is ₹127.2338 crore against equity ₹406.1678 crore; operating EBIT covers reported interest about 18 times.; Only ₹1.3452 crore is unrestricted CCE; ₹79.9632 crore deposits are restricted and guarantee exposures are material. Contrary: CRISIL A-/Positive and moderate trailing utilization suggest flexibility, but rated lines are not unconditional available cash and the rating assumes no large debt-funded capex. | ar2026, rating2026, transcript2026 |\\n| EXEC | 6 | Medium | FY25–26 EPC revenue and management-reported physical deliveries grew substantially.; ₹25.1093 crore of original equipment, solar and hydrogen IPO objects remained unspent at 8 August 2026, with redirection proposed to BESS. Contrary: Delivery in EPC is demonstrated; new owned-asset commissioning and the promised cash collection improvement are not yet demonstrated. | ar2026, transcript2026, presentation2026 |\\n| EARN | 7 | Medium | FY26 operating EBIT is ₹196.0215 crore after excluding other income and JV profit and adding back the identified ₹0.6392 crore exception.; Reported diluted EPS increased from ₹59.75 to ₹79.52; FY26 issued shares remained 18,007,392 after the IPO. Contrary: Deferred-tax credit of ₹6.4847 crore and JV profit of ₹5.6911 crore affect PAT. Input-method estimates, mix and historical other income limit pure recurring-earnings confidence. | ar2026, prospectus |\\n| R | NS | Low | The five-year monthly cash model suppresses returns after any opening, pre-entry, intraperiod or terminal funding breach.; Actual entry cash and committed BESS finance remain unverified; the quoted close is only secondary-corroborated. Contrary: A cash-surviving conditional model is useful, but cannot become a supported central investment-return score. | ar2026, transcript2026, model |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | Joint collection, supplier-credit, margin and guarantee stress creates finite funding shortfalls.; Owned BESS, restricted margin deposits and connected guarantees can force adverse recapitalization before reported earnings collapse. Contrary: Moderate reported leverage, audited assets and established utility customers offer recovery support. Stress failure does not prove current default or zero recovery. | ar2026, rating2026, model |\\n| P | NS | Low | Reverse growth tests preserve every failed candidate and distinguish funding boundaries from valid return roots.; Price-implied growth depends on future collections, capital intensity and project funding that are not verified at entry. Contrary: Headline P/E appears modest, but cannot on its own resolve retained cash, new project capital or terminal funding requirements. | model, ar2026, market_secondary |\\n| L | NS | Low | The official 19 October security master gives 100-share lots and 18,007,392 shares.; Official BSE 20/60-session trading data, spreads and current restrictions were not retrieved; one secondary 28,200-share print is insufficient. Contrary: Trading is quoted and no suspension is established. Missing BSE rows or NSE absence must not be interpreted as zero volume. | security, market_secondary |\\n\\nComposite sensitivity, with all missing factors preserved and overrides reapplied:\\n\\n| Changed factor | Score shift | BQS | GQS | IAS |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| GOV | -1 | 5.7 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| GOV | 1 | 6.1 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| MOAT | -1 | 5.7 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| MOAT | 1 | 6.1 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CAP | -1 | 5.8 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CAP | 1 | 6.0 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CASH | -1 | 5.8 | 5.0 | NS |\\n| CASH | 1 | 6.0 | 7.1 | NS |\\n\\nA score change alone cannot close the funding or trading gates. In particular, a CASH increase to 4 would require new evidence that persistent poor realization has improved; it is not a discretionary way to remove the cap.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nRajesh executes underground cable systems, substations, distribution infrastructure and associated testing, commissioning and maintenance. It traces operating history to 1971, with the present company incorporated in 2010. FY26 operating revenue was ₹1,627.94 crore, overwhelmingly EPC; ₹1,627.47 crore was EPC revenue and ₹0.47 crore other operating revenue. Own solar generation is not the basis of the growth thesis. EPC revenue follows progress/input-cost accounting, while cash follows billing certification, customer payments, advance recovery and retention release. Recognition is therefore not equivalent to collection. Discounts/rebates of ₹21.58 crore are already deducted from contract revenue; do not add them back as normal sales. [FY26 annual report, revenue policy and notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf).\\n\\nThe same industry label conceals three economic models:\\n\\n1. **EPC:** qualify, win, procure, execute, certify, bill, collect. Supplier credit finances part of the work; guarantees and retentions consume capital.\\n2. **Joint ventures:** equity-accounted profit is not parent cash. HKRP and RPSL-VITS carrying value is separated from EPC valuation; no double credit for JV earnings plus its full asset value.\\n3. **Owned BESS:** pay to build and maintain a storage asset, then earn finite capacity charges subject to availability and performance. No fictional EPC sale or internal construction margin is booked on the company's own asset in this model.\\n\\nTwo clients represented 56.10% of FY26 sales. CRISIL reported 93% Gujarat / 7% Odisha June backlog and 90% of backlog with six customers. The utility customer base may reduce some commercial-credit uncertainty but does not remove certification delays, retention conditions, concentration or public-sector procurement risk. [July rating](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html).\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\n**MOAT 6:** execution qualifications, experienced crews, local delivery knowledge and relationships create bidding access, not monopoly pricing. Management described roughly 1,300 km of cable, 4,000 RMUs, 1,200 transformers and 350 feeder panels in FY26; these are management-reported delivery measures rather than independently certified physical audits. Escalation clauses help mitigate input-price exposure but can also inflate reported revenue without equivalent real-volume growth. Geographic expansion must reproduce site execution and collection, not just win tenders. [April earnings call](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Transcription-H2FY26-Earnings-Call-on-23.04.2026.pdf).\\n\\n| Comparator | Relevant economic comparison | Limitation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| KEC International | Global T&D plus other infrastructure; FY25 consolidated revenue ₹21,847 crore, EBITDA margin 7.0%, and NWC 122 days in its dated earnings release. Demonstrates that scale does not eliminate working-capital pressure. | Far larger, diversified and internationally exposed. FY25 EBITDA included ₹24 crore arbitration receipt; no automatic peer multiple applied. |\\n| Transrail Lighting | T&D EPC with tower/conductor manufacturing, substations and other infrastructure. A relevant capability and vertical-integration benchmark. | Different mix and geography; no unverified contemporaneous valuation comparison. |\\n| Kalpataru Projects International | Diversified project execution is a relevant competitive benchmark, not the similarly named real-estate company. | Current comparable financial/multiple dataset not completed; no numerical peer premium inferred. |\\n\\nSources: [KEC FY25 primary earnings release](https://www.kecrpg.com/press-release/earnings/financial-performance-for-quarter-%26-year-ended-31-march-2025-26-05-25), [Transrail primary business and annual-report index](https://transrail.in/investors/financial-reports/annual-reports/), [KPIL primary investor index](https://www.kalpataruprojects.com/investors/financials/annual-reports). These are business comparisons, not a claim that peer fair values validate Rajesh's price.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\n| Years | Period | Starting revenue ₹cr | Ending revenue ₹cr | CAGR |\\n|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 3 | 2023–2026 | 207.18 | 1,627.94 | 98.81% |\\n| 5 | 2021–2026 | 111.60 | 1,627.94 | 70.92% |\\n| 6 | 2020–2026 | 167.72 | 1,627.94 | 46.05% |\\n\\n| Fiscal year | Revenue addition ₹cr | Growth |\\n|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 2021 | -56.12 | -33.46% |\\n| 2022 | 35.21 | 31.55% |\\n| 2023 | 60.37 | 41.12% |\\n| 2024 | 77.79 | 37.55% |\\n| 2025 | 787.10 | 276.20% |\\n| 2026 | 555.87 | 51.85% |\\n\\nThe FY21 pandemic trough exaggerates five-year CAGR, but the FY20–26 pre-pandemic cross-check is also strong. FY26 growth over the **restated** FY25 base was 51.85%; H2 FY26 revenue ₹990.12 crore versus ₹759.01 crore was approximately 30.45% higher. The evidence supports genuine expansion in project activity, not a pure price or accounting story. It does **not** quantify the exact split between cable/input inflation, mix, work-volume and pricing. No acquisition contribution is assumed to explain the EPC surge; a precise constant-price organic CAGR remains ND. [Presentation](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Investor-Presentation-RPSL-H2FY26.pdf), [prospectus](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Prospectus_RPSL.pdf).\\n\\n**Comparability control:** original FY25 consolidated revenue ₹1,107.44 crore / PAT ₹93.37 crore changed to ₹1,072.07 crore / ₹96.63 crore under Ind AS, primarily with equity accounting of the JV. FY26 comparisons use the restated figures. Older standalone EPC revenue is economically cross-checked against the later scope, not falsely labelled a fully restated six-year Ind AS series. The revenue schedule starts at 9 for CAGR≥30%; the assigned **8** reflects Medium confidence and basis/driver limits without multiplying arbitrary penalties for the same pandemic base.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nMarch 2026 remaining performance obligations were ₹3,326 crore, with recognition over **one to five years**, not all within 18 months. June unexecuted orders were ₹3,164 crore in CRISIL's July report. These are dated snapshots, not additive amounts. Secondary October announcements of ₹471.92 crore and ₹69.004 crore include taxes; they are not mechanically added to the older backlog without execution, cancellation and GST reconciliation. [FY26 annual report](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf), [rating](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html).\\n\\nA bottom-up growth path requires qualified labour/subcontractors, procurement capacity, utility work fronts, incremental receivables and retention funding, BG lines and cash margin. FY25–26 funded operating capital rose ₹216.44 crore while normalized NOPAT rose ₹54.89 crore, an accrued incremental proxy of 25.36%. This is promising, but collected cash and new-asset returns are different tests. The base forecast explicitly finances rising working capital and collateral from operations rather than converting backlog directly to free cash flow.\\n\\n### BESS: finite asset, not a free growth option\\n\\nThe 65 MW / 130 MWh project at ₹1.89 lakh/MW/month produces **₹14.742 crore annual gross capacity charges**, ₹176.904 crore over twelve years before costs. Management's ₹1.5–2.5 crore/MW cost range implies ₹97.5–162.5 crore project capital. The ₹25.1093 crore redirected IPO allocation is only one equity/internal contribution, not total project cost. Discussions for 70–80% bank finance are not a sanction. Management's indicated 10–12% IRR is a statement, not an independently verified return. [Issuer BESS update](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/20260306_%20Press%20Release%20for%20BESS%20update_.pdf), [earnings call](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Transcription-H2FY26-Earnings-Call-on-23.04.2026.pdf).\\n\\nEven with **zero** operating cost, tax and replacement, twelve-year monthly tariff PV at commissioning is only ₹96.24 crore at 12% and ₹85.27 crore at 15%, below the low ₹97.5 crore gross capex case. This is a useful upper-bound test, **not** proof the subsidized project cannot work. The Phase VII RfS provides potential VGF of ₹18 lakh/MWh, or **₹23.4 crore**, released 20% at financial closure, 50% at COD and 30% one year later, subject to certification and government receipts. A separate BG equal to the entire grant is needed before the first tranche. At an assumed 25% margin, ₹5.85 crore is tied up before a ₹4.68 crore first payment; 100% collateral and payment delay are also stressed. Issuer-specific sanction and unchanged signed BESPA terms remain ND. [Original GUVNL RfS, mirrored document, sections 8–12 and 19–23](https://solarquarter.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/1753286163254.pdf).\\n\\nUsing the disclosed 6 March 2026 BESPA date, the original RfS implies financial closure by 6 December 2026 and COD by 6 September 2027, subject to any signed amendments. The conditional model assumes closure on 19 November 2026, not an uncharged late closure. September 19 is a reporting-bucket end, **not** a shifted COD date. Actual-day overlap allocates each of the 144 contractual monthly periods into reporting buckets; tariff, depreciation and operating costs share that same schedule.\\n\\nThe model's ordinary base recognizes **zero unverified grant receipts**. Separate conditional branches recognize the grant with three- and twelve-month delays, collateral and lower depreciable project basis. Availability, round-trip efficiency and augmentation can further reduce cash. The RfS calls for 95% availability, 85% round-trip efficiency, performance deductions and developer-funded augmentation; a ₹30 crore year-eight replacement is an analytical stress, not a vendor quote. The twelve-month delay bear assumes termination rather than silently keeping full tariff cash after the RfS's nine-month delay window. Existing PBG exposure is not added twice to normal BG outstanding.\\n\\n**GQS 5.0 capped:** the runway is substantial, but repeated negative cash realization and uncertain asset financing prevent a high growth-quality conclusion.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nPromoters controlled 72.70% at March 2026. The IPO combined ₹93.465 crore fresh issuance with ₹67 crore OFS: OFS belongs to selling shareholders, not the company. Authorized capital permits 25 million shares, but authorization is not permission, demand or cash for a future issue. The model's rescue is deliberately hypothetical and bounded.\\n\\n| Commitment / observation | Dated evidence | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Original IPO equipment, solar and hydrogen projects | ₹25.1093 crore remained unspent as of 8 August 2026; proposed redirection to BESS | Allocation slippage and changed risk mix, not evidence of cash theft |\\n| FY26 EPC delivery | Revenue and management-reported physical execution expanded | Positive execution evidence; collection still lags |\\n| BESS commissioning | Management indicated September 2027 | Monitor actual financial closure, equipment ordering, commissioning and grant certification |\\n| Bank funding | Management discussed 70–80% project debt | Not committed; no lender term sheet or full project funding accepted as available |\\n| New Zealand subsidiary | Incorporation confirmed 14 August; filing dated 15 August 2026 | New geographic option, not awarded revenue or proven overseas economics |\\n| Auditor change | FY26 mandatory rotation | Not characterized as a suspicious resignation |\\n\\nFY21–26 full original main audit opinions, CARO where applicable and IFC annexures were reviewed, not merely the prospectus restatement certificate. Opinions were unmodified; latest audit trail operation and preservation were reported. Revenue recognized using costs/progress was a FY26 key audit matter. Reliance on other subsidiary/JV auditors is disclosed, not concealed. Audit cleanliness does not establish receivable collectability or the quality of future capex. [Annual reports in section 15].\\n\\nGovernance watch items include ₹99.2185 crore **sanctioned** co-guarantees for sister concerns, ₹76.3426 crore related-party purchases, four executive directors’ remuneration of ₹8.21 crore (excluding additional related-person salaries), and loans declining from ₹13.1078 crore to ₹6.2417 crore. Sanctioned guarantee face value is neither automatically debt outstanding nor a current cash payment. FY22–24 CARO wording says both that repayment schedules are stipulated and that all loans are on demand/without specified terms; the contradiction is retained. Earlier footer CIN inconsistencies and preferential-allotment boilerplate were checked against current identity/share capital rather than treated as a different issuer or fabricated dilution. CRISIL also preserves a 2021 India Ratings non-cooperation history for missing monitoring information. No substantiated diversion or severe governance breach was established.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\nAll monetary amounts below are ₹ crore. Earlier rows have their own basis; do not sum them as a clean, comparable cash series.\\n\\n| FY | Basis | Revenue | Operating EBITDA | PAT | CFO | CFO less cash capex | Debt | Equity |\\n|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 2020 | Original standalone IGAAP comparative only | 167.72 | 7.00 | 2.49 | 12.31 | 10.90 | 28.55 | 46.75 |\\n| 2021 | Original standalone IGAAP; original CFO classification | 111.60 | 10.60 | 4.37 | 15.88 | 15.36 | 48.32 | 49.22 |\\n| 2022 | IPO restated standalone IGAAP | 146.81 | 11.03 | 3.45 | 6.52 | 5.03 | 62.43 | 52.29 |\\n| 2023 | IPO restated standalone IGAAP | 207.18 | 14.01 | 6.75 | 15.36 | 11.77 | 59.71 | 58.66 |\\n| 2024 | IPO restated standalone IGAAP | 284.97 | 33.96 | 26.02 | -14.66 | -16.18 | 77.67 | 84.30 |\\n| 2025 | FY26 Ind AS restated consolidated; JV equity method | 1,072.07 | 123.89 | 96.63 | -26.70 | -28.37 | 77.86 | 264.88 |\\n| 2026 | Audited consolidated Ind AS; JV equity method | 1,627.94 | 197.16 | 143.20 | -41.42 | -43.60 | 127.23 | 406.17 |\\n\\n### Earnings to cash\\n\\nFY26 reported PBT ₹195.6423 crore plus finance ₹10.9013 crore, less other income ₹5.4702 crore and JV profit ₹5.6911 crore, plus identified labour-code exception ₹0.6392 crore, gives normalized **operating EBIT ₹196.0215 crore**, EBITDA ₹197.1591 crore. A 25% normalized tax assumption gives NOPAT ₹147.0161 crore. It is not a forecast of actual tax. Reported PAT includes a ₹6.4847 crore deferred-tax credit and ₹4.0834 crore prior-year tax charge. The JV CFS adjustment ₹5.6973 crore differs from P&L ₹5.6911 crore by ₹0.0062 crore; unresolved but immaterial to the conclusion.\\n\\nCash generation did not follow profit. FY26 receivables absorbed ₹171.1229 crore, other financial assets ₹161.0640 crore and inventory ₹62.0980 crore; trade payables supplied ₹207.8024 crore. CFO was **−₹41.4185 crore**, even before ₹2.1837 crore cash fixed/intangible capex and ₹26.4862 crore investing cash deposited with banks. FY25–26 CFO/PAT was **-28.40%**. “No cash losses” in CARO refers to accounting cash-profit tests, not positive operating cash flow.\\n\\nFY26 unrestricted CCE is **₹1.3452 crore**. Current bank margin deposits ₹64.1066 crore plus noncurrent ₹15.8566 crore total **₹79.9632 crore** and are retained as restricted operating capital. ₹348.7721 crore current receivables and ₹279.4349 crore retention together equal about 141 days of FY26 sales on closing balances. Retention release can follow six-month to two-year defect-liability periods. These cannot be treated as cash equivalents. Inventory ₹124.9462 crore is financed partly by ₹328.3156 crore payables and ₹25.0565 crore customer advances. Inventory/payable days and a conventional CCC are ND rather than calculated with an incompatible sales denominator.\\n\\n### Funded-capital reconciliation and incremental return\\n\\nOperating IC = equity ₹406.1678 + debt ₹127.2338 − free cash ₹1.3452 − JV ₹23.1474 − all loans ₹6.7871 − deferred-tax asset ₹8.8713 + current tax liability ₹10.1630 = **₹503.4136 crore**. Asset-side bridge: narrow ONWC ₹399.7811 + residual operating-net items ₹4.4084 + restricted deposits ₹79.9632 + fixed/CWIP ₹19.2609 = the same total. The residual is a financing reconciliation and includes a tiny unpaid-dividend classification; it is not a fully itemized “pure” trade-working-capital account. Unverified recoveries and restricted deposit release are not added to cash.\\n\\nFY25 IC ₹286.9773 crore and opening FY24 Ind AS IC ₹141.6979 crore anchor the comparison. FY26 NOPAT / average FY25–26 IC = **37.20%**. Incremental FY25–26 NOPAT / incremental IC = **25.36%**; the two-year mixed-basis proxy = **33.75%**. **Three- and five-year incremental-capital windows are ND** because older cash/deposit classification and consolidation bases are not sufficiently reconciled; the one- and two-year proxies are not relabelled as substitutes. These are accrual return proxies, not project IRRs or proof new capex itself earned the entire profit uplift. Margin/mix gains on old operations, low book PPE, supplier funding and uncollected retention all matter. The CAP score therefore remains 7, not 9–10.\\n\\n### Debt, guarantees and accounting vintages\\n\\nDebt ₹127.2338 crore comprises current ₹81.5882 crore and noncurrent ₹45.6456 crore. Related-party debt ₹45.0023 crore is not converted to equity in the model; CRISIL's treatment of 75% of ₹43.34 crore promoter loans as quasi-equity is a rating adjustment, not legal extinguishment. Agreements defer related-loan repayment until after 31 March 2028. Scenario repayment dates for facilities are explicit assumptions, not invented maturities. Bank guarantees are ₹312.7171 crore. CRISIL's ₹151 crore CC / ₹212 crore BG ratings and 34.04% trailing average utilization do not establish drawable October headroom. Its rationale assumes absence of large/debt-funded capex, which must be reconciled with BESS rather than treated as its underwriting.\\n\\nOriginal versus later CFO presentations are preserved: FY21 original ₹15.8778 crore versus FY22 comparative ₹13.9185; FY22 original **−₹10.8969** versus IPO-restated **+₹6.5191**; FY23 original ₹17.2187 versus restated ₹15.3579; FY24 original **−₹25.3843**, IPO-restated **−₹14.6624** and FY25 comparative **−₹22.1081**. The FY24 restatement moves approximately ₹10.7217 crore to noncurrent-asset investing outflow. No cumulative five-year CFO ratio is presented as comparable.\\n\\nCash-flow net IPO proceeds ₹85.3825 crore deduct ₹8.0825 crore issue expenses, whereas objects net ₹77.9693 crore deduct ₹15.4957 crore overall costs. Allocation/timing needs reconciliation. The CARO statement that ₹25.1093 crore unspent IPO proceeds were parked in the **cash-credit account** means the amount is not independently added as a second cash asset. Current CCE and drawn CC already reflect the balance-sheet position. The FY25 weighted average EPS denominator is **16,170,642**; FY26 actual/weighted shares are 18,007,392. Using ₹59.75→₹79.52 reported EPS avoids dividing both periods by the wrong denominator.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\nAt ₹821.85, market cap is ₹1,479.94 crore, reported FY26 P/E **10.33×**, normalized operating earnings P/E **10.66×**, book P/B **3.64×**. Historical EV uses debt less only free CCE, not restricted deposits. After separately removing JV and nonoperating loan carrying values, illustrative EPC EV/EBITDA is **8.00×**, EV/EBIT **8.04×**. These are March-balance-sheet multiples at the October price, not verified October net-debt multiples. The reported earnings yield is not a distributable cash yield.\\n\\n### Linked cash model\\n\\nThe model starts at audited 31 March 2026 cash and carries seven monthly pre-entry periods to 19 October, then 60 monthly periods. Opening cash is tested before any receipts. Each period shows early operating receipts, working capital, collateral, EPC maintenance/growth capex, BESS, tax, interest, maturities and late receipts. There is no opening-cash reset at entry. The base reaches **₹59.80 crore modeled entry cash**, but this depends on collection/retention normalization and is **not observed October cash**.\\n\\n| Input | Bear | Base | Bull |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Pre-entry annualized sales / EBITDA margin | ₹1,450cr / 7.5% | ₹1,800cr / 11.5% | ₹2,000cr / 12.5% |\\n| Five forecast annual sales ₹cr | 1,450; 1,370; 1,440; 1,550; 1,650 | 2,000; 2,280; 2,550; 2,800; 3,000 | 2,200; 2,700; 3,250; 3,800; 4,300 |\\n| EBITDA margins | 7.5%; 7%; 7.5%; 8%; 8% | 11% throughout | 12.5%; 12.5%; 12%; 12%; 12% |\\n| AR / retention / inventory, % sales | 27 / 22 / 9 | 19 / 16 / 7.5 | 17 / 14 / 7 |\\n| Payables / advances, % sales | 16 / 1.2 | 20 / 1.5 | 21 / 1.7 |\\n| Restricted deposits, % sales floor | 5.5 | 5.0 | 4.5 |\\n| BESS total / operating cost / annual maintenance ₹cr | 162.5 / 4.0 / 1.5 | 130 / 3.0 / 1.0 | 97.5 / 2.5 / 0.8 |\\n| BESS delay | 12 months, termination stress | No delay | No delay |\\n\\nAll these are A. Receipts are 70% before requirements and 30% after, stressed to 30%/100%. Cash floor ₹1 crore is an assumption tested at ₹5/10 crore; it is not a bank covenant. No restricted collateral is automatically released on slowing sales. Historical tax payable, pre-entry dividend, loan interest, debt run-off, default guarantee stress and whole-share dilution all flow through cash.\\n\\n| Path | Cash survives? | Minimum cash ₹cr | Year-five cash ₹cr | Earned EPC terminal EV ₹cr | Finite BESS terminal EV ₹cr | Five-year scenario IRR |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| bear_none | No | -400.70 | -224.31 | 660.90 | 0.00 | NS |\\n| bear_bounded_optional | No | -400.70 | -224.31 | 660.90 | 0.00 | NS |\\n| base_none | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 562.00 | 2,065.14 | 32.59 | 12.67% |\\n| base_bounded_optional | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 562.00 | 2,065.14 | 32.59 | 12.67% |\\n| bull_none | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 956.92 | 3,361.54 | 32.10 | 24.23% |\\n| bull_bounded_optional | Yes (assumed) | 1.35 | 956.92 | 3,361.54 | 32.10 | 24.23% |\\n\\nA cash breach is sticky. Negative balances may continue in the diagnostic ledger to expose the size of the financing gap, but they do not become ordinary returns or an executable recovery value. Optional bank/equity paths never repair a pre-entry breach. The zero-new-finance base and bull assume sufficient internally realized cash; that result is conditional, not evidence that a lender has committed funds.\\n\\nBase annual cash bridge:\\n\\n| Forecast year | Sales | EPC EBITDA | ΔONWC | Deposits added | EPC capex | BESS capex | Tax paid | Interest | Debt repaid | Closing cash |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 1 | 2,000.00 | 220.00 | 42.60 | 10.00 | 4.14 | 115.73 | 51.56 | 12.72 | 0.00 | 47.15 |\\n| 2 | 2,280.00 | 250.80 | 59.64 | 14.00 | 5.48 | 0.00 | 59.43 | 12.72 | 127.23 | 33.39 |\\n| 3 | 2,550.00 | 280.50 | 57.51 | 13.50 | 5.49 | 0.00 | 69.99 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 181.74 |\\n| 4 | 2,800.00 | 308.00 | 53.25 | 12.50 | 5.35 | 0.00 | 76.83 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 356.55 |\\n| 5 | 3,000.00 | 330.00 | 42.60 | 10.00 | 4.74 | 0.00 | 82.29 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 562.00 |\\n\\n### Earned terminal value, not an assumed exit multiple\\n\\nEPC terminal FCFF uses actual modeled capital, maintenance equal to modeled depreciation, full incremental receivables/retentions/inventory less operating liabilities, restricted deposits and fixed investment. At base 4% growth / 15% discount, next-year NOPAT is **₹256.03 crore**, operating capital **₹834.00 crore**, incremental growth investment **₹33.36 crore**, and FCFF **₹227.17 crore**. Implied NOPAT / terminal capital is **30.70%**, reported as an earned model result rather than a freely chosen target ROIC. EPC FCFF/(discount−growth) is primary; no arbitrary P/E or EV/EBITDA rerating creates the return.\\n\\nBESS receives only the remaining twelve-year contract cash flows, with maintenance and augmentation; no perpetual terminal tariff, salvage or automatic renewal. JV is valued once at ₹23.1474 crore carrying value; nonoperating loans receive 50%/80%/100% recovery assumptions. No JV profit enters EPC EBITDA or cash flow. A further twelve-month continuation retains all monthly operating, BESS, debt and capital requirements; terminal reserve equals the maximum cumulative funding gap plus the cash floor. Only surplus cash above that reserve enters equity. Base reserve is **₹1.00 crore**. No operating working capital or BG deposits are also liquidated into terminal equity.\\n\\n| Discount rate | Terminal EPC growth | Conditional terminal price ₹ | Scenario IRR | Entry unlevered DCF cross-check ₹/share |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 12.00% | 2.00% | 1,673.49 | 15.28% | 980.11 |\\n| 12.00% | 4.00% | 1,923.66 | 18.54% | 1,122.06 |\\n| 12.00% | 6.00% | 2,340.61 | 23.28% | 1,358.65 |\\n| 15.00% | 2.00% | 1,365.82 | 10.69% | 711.12 |\\n| 15.00% | 4.00% | 1,492.09 | 12.67% | 773.91 |\\n| 15.00% | 6.00% | 1,674.49 | 15.30% | 864.59 |\\n| 18.00% | 2.00% | 1,173.19 | 7.38% | 545.67 |\\n| 18.00% | 4.00% | 1,245.07 | 8.66% | 577.09 |\\n| 18.00% | 6.00% | 1,340.91 | 10.29% | 618.98 |\\n\\nThe terminal ledger separately reports whether forward-year net cash is nonnegative after all requirements; inherited cash sufficient for one year does not establish perpetual self-funding. The 12/15/18% discount grid is an analytical nominal-INR risk sensitivity, **not** a precisely estimated WACC and not automatically equal to the equity hurdle. The DCF cross-check discounts actual modeled five-year unlevered FCFF and earned terminal operating value, then adjusts entry cash/debt and separately valued assets. For optional financing it is not old-shareholder value without explicit financier-claim valuation. Conditional terminal wealth includes dilution, fees and remaining debt. Terminal value dependence, collection improvement and BESS cost are material model risks.\\n\\nConditional base terminal value supports these five-year hurdle-entry arithmetic levels, **not buy prices** while G3/G4 remain unresolved:\\n\\n| Equity hurdle | Conditional entry ₹ |\\n|---:|---:|\\n| 12.00% | 846.65 |\\n| 15.00% | 741.83 |\\n| 18.00% | 652.21 |\\n\\nReverse tests solve annual sales CAGR from a ₹1,800 crore starting run-rate while fixing base margin/capital ratios. Each tested candidate recomputes the full cash path. The search domain is 0–60% CAGR; failed funding candidates are retained, and a financing boundary is never called a return root.\\n\\n| Target | Five-year wealth multiple | Retained tests | Cash-surviving growth root / result |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| 0.12 | 1.76 | 39 | 10.10%–10.10% |\\n| 0.15 | 2.01 | 39 | 14.22%–14.22% |\\n| 0.18 | 2.29 | 13 | No cash-surviving sign-change found |\\n| 5x | 5.00 | 13 | No cash-surviving sign-change found |\\n| 10x | 10.00 | 13 | No cash-surviving sign-change found |\\n\\nSecondary multiple-normalization diagnostics keep the identical cash path, restricted-capital reserve, finite BESS value, financing and dilution. The unchanged historical EPC multiple is 8.00×; 6× normalization and 4× compression do not require rerating. These do not replace the primary earned-FCFF valuation.\\n\\n| Path | Multiple case | EPC EV/EBITDA | Diagnostic terminal price ₹ | Conditional IRR |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| bear_none | compression | 4.00 | 307.80 | NS |\\n| bear_none | normalization | 6.00 | 454.41 | NS |\\n| bear_none | unchanged_historical_EPC_multiple | 8.00 | 600.70 | NS |\\n| base_none | compression | 4.00 | 1,078.30 | 5.58% |\\n| base_none | normalization | 6.00 | 1,444.81 | 11.94% |\\n| base_none | unchanged_historical_EPC_multiple | 8.00 | 1,810.55 | 17.11% |\\n| bull_none | compression | 4.00 | 1,711.19 | 15.80% |\\n| bull_none | normalization | 6.00 | 2,284.29 | 22.69% |\\n| bull_none | unchanged_historical_EPC_multiple | 8.00 | 2,856.18 | 28.29% |\\n\\nAt the primary base discount/growth, five-year operating FCFF PV is **₹389.95 crore**, terminal operating value PV is **₹1,042.94 crore**, and terminal value supplies **72.79%** of the operating enterprise DCF. Nonoperating assets and entry cash/debt are outside that denominator. Dependence can exceed 100% in cash-consuming cases; it is not a probability or assurance.\\n\\nExecution costs, spread and market impact cannot be precisely verified without the BSE tape and order book. The following analytical sensitivities charge both entry and exit cash flows, retain zero interim dividends, and are before investor tax; they are not claims of executable transaction costs.\\n\\n| Entry cost | Exit cost | Entry paid ₹/share | Terminal net ₹/share | Base IRR after costs |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 0.00% | 0.00% | 821.85 | 1,492.09 | 12.67% |\\n| 0.50% | 0.50% | 825.96 | 1,484.63 | 12.44% |\\n| 1.00% | 1.00% | 830.07 | 1,477.17 | 12.22% |\\n| 2.00% | 2.00% | 838.29 | 1,462.25 | 11.77% |\\n\\nThe archive also retains the full 3×3 margin/collection grid, 3×3 discount/growth grid, ₹5/10 crore cash-floor tests, receipt timing, ₹97.5/162.5 crore BESS capex, CWIP allocation, guarantee calls, VGF collateral/delay and BESS-delay tests. There are **91 unique cash paths and 149 test references**; exact duplicates share a complete path rather than disappear. All failed paths, brackets and terminal continuations are preserved.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe strongest bear case is not “orders stop growing.” It is **recognized growth keeps consuming cash while customer collections slow and supplier finance tightens**, at the same time that BESS requires equity, bank guarantees and capex. Margin compression can reduce cash available just as operating capital expands. A guarantee call or related-party exposure could force financing before the accounting loss becomes visible.\\n\\nThe linked bear uses lower sales/margins, 27% AR plus 22% retention, reduced supplier support, 5% receivable/retention loss funding, 10% of other bank guarantees (excluding the separately called ₹8.125 crore BESS PBG) and 20% co-guarantee cash calls, BESS overrun/termination, and debt run-off. The early cash breach cannot be repaired by hypothetical equity arriving after entry. Asset recovery and creditor priority are uncertain; no unsupported “zero recovery” or neat negative IRR is supplied.\\n\\nSeparate base-path guarantee stresses test the rescue mechanism. New debt is capped at ₹100 crore gross, charges 11% including a full issue-month interest reserve, and amortizes without assumed refinancing. Equity is capped at ₹100 crore gross at ₹657.48/share, with 3% fees, integer shares and authorized-share headroom. Neither is committed. The severe ₹411.9356 crore call is a full-face exposure stress, **not** an assertion that all guarantees will be called or all co-guaranteed debt is drawn.\\n\\n| Stress / financing | Survives? | Optional bank ₹cr | Optional equity gross ₹cr | Final shares | Dilution |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| guarantee_shock_150_none | No; returns NS | 0.00 | 0.00 | 18007392 | 0.00% |\\n| guarantee_shock_150_bounded_optional | Yes, conditional | 100.00 | 37.45 | 18577044 | 3.07% |\\n| guarantee_shock_411.9356_none | No; returns NS | 0.00 | 0.00 | 18007392 | 0.00% |\\n| guarantee_shock_411.9356_bounded_optional | No; returns NS | 100.00 | 100.00 | 19528350 | 7.79% |\\n\\nThesis-break variable: **cash tied up per additional rupee of executed revenue**, including retentions and collateral, relative to credible cash earnings and drawable finance. Recovery of old receivables without replacement by larger new retentions is more decisive than an order announcement.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nFivefold wealth in five years requires approximately **37.97% CAGR**; tenfold requires **58.49%** before investor tax. They are outcomes to test, not forecasts. At unchanged shares and no dividends, fivefold price means ₹4,109.25 and tenfold ₹8,218.50, with equity values roughly ₹7,400 crore and ₹14,799 crore before dilution. The current audited scale does not make either automatic.\\n\\nThe cash-linked fixed-margin reverse tests above found no cash-surviving sign change for 5× or 10× within the tested 0–60% revenue-CAGR range. That is **not a mathematical proof of impossibility outside the tested assumptions**: margins, capital efficiency, grants, new equity or business mix could change. But funded scaling, per-share dilution and terminal reinvestment must change with them. BESS's ₹14.742 crore annual gross tariff cannot by itself justify a multi-thousand-crore equity rerating. A possible exceptional outcome is not a scoreable base thesis.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\n| KPI / baseline | Evidence to obtain | Warning / thesis-break threshold |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Free cash ₹1.3452cr at March 2026 | September cash flow, bank balance classification and actual October bridge | No verified drawable cash to meet essential payments; do not infer from bank deposits |\\n| FY25–26 CFO/PAT negative | Half-year and full-year collections, retention releases and aged balances | CFO remains negative despite slower growth, or operating capital rises faster than accrued profit |\\n| AR + retention ₹628.2070cr | Customer/project ageing, certification and defect-liability dates | Material overdue/uncertified balances, disputed claims or retention release slippage |\\n| Restricted deposits ₹79.9632cr | BG margin terms, expiry, utilization and release permissions | Collateral demand outpaces available cash; no automatic margin release assumed |\\n| Debt ₹127.2338cr | Sanctions, covenants, renewal, repayment schedule and undrawn availability | Renewal denied or callable debt cannot be serviced without unsupported rescue |\\n| BESS total cost / finance ND | Signed EPC/battery contract, funding tie-up and grant sanction | Cost above ₹162.5cr or closure delayed without funded contingency; contract delay/termination exposure |\\n| VGF potential ₹23.4cr | CEA certification, government receipt and BG cash-margin terms | First receipt delayed while full BG cash margin is locked; no grant double count |\\n| BESS availability / augmentation | Signed performance schedule, warranties, replacement budget | Availability/efficiency deductions or augmentation exceed model cash margins |\\n| Related guarantees ₹99.2185cr sanctioned | Actual debt drawn, beneficiary health and releases | Connected exposure rises faster than capital or is called without reimbursement |\\n| BSE lot 100; 60-session tape ND | Official daily volume/value, no-trade days, spreads, circuit/restriction notices | Reference exit exceeds acceptable participation capacity or effectively cannot execute |\\n\\nA potential catalyst is evidence, not merely an announcement: improved cash conversion, verified project closure, on-time commissioning or demonstrated geographic diversification. Failure to obtain evidence keeps the relevant gate unresolved.\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Hold — unresolved funding and tradability; pending evidence.** Not Avoid–governance, not a finding of insolvency, and not a low-IAS stock automatically called expensive. BQS 5.9 reflects useful but contestable execution capability. GQS 5.0 reflects the cash override despite strong growth. IAS remains NS because R/P/L cannot be defensibly scored at the reference price with current evidence. The scoreable BQS/GQS arithmetic can be compared descriptively, but this gated record should not be promoted into investable rankings.\\n\\nRajesh does not meet compounder thresholds of BQS≥8 and GQS≥7, irrespective of any future price gain. A lower price can improve modeled returns, but cannot establish free cash, a lender commitment, subsidy receipt or market liquidity. Reassess only after the specific funding and trading gaps are closed and the actual post-March cash bridge replaces assumptions. Nothing here authorizes or recommends a trade or a portfolio allocation.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n**Evidence legend:** V = verified public historical fact; M = management statement; A = analyst assumption/output; ND = not disclosed or verified; NS = not scoreable. Original issuer PDFs, OCR, fetched web bodies, transfer helpers and private service identifiers are not redistributed. Source URLs, hashes where bytes were obtained, page references, authored factual extracts, model code and full calculation rows are retained. Hashes verify the retrieved edition, not authenticity beyond the source provenance.\\n\\nKey assumptions are deliberately exposed: the March-to-October cash bridge; annual sales and margins; receipt timing; receivable/retention/payable intensity; deposit margins; maintenance and replacement; debt run-off; 25% normalized tax; hypothetical rescue price/caps; VGF certification delay and collateral; 12/15/18% discount; 2/4/6% terminal growth; JV/loan recovery and no post-entry dividends. ₹1 crore plant-and-machinery CWIP is **not explicitly identified as BESS** in the audit; base allocation is A and a zero-BESS-allocation alternative is retained. Fees, source vintage differences and unknown exact maturity schedules are not invented away.\\n\\nOpen questions with the greatest decision impact:\\n\\n1. What are unrestricted cash and genuinely drawable, covenant-compliant facilities at entry, after all BG margins and committed capex?\\n2. How much of the FY26 receivable and retention balance has actually been collected, and how much was replaced by new locked capital?\\n3. What are binding BESS total cost, availability/efficiency deductions, augmentation obligations, warranties and exact contractual commissioning dates?\\n4. Is issuer-specific VGF sanctioned, what are certification and disbursement dates, and how much BG collateral is required? Does the final BESPA amend the original RfS?\\n5. How do IPO expense allocation and cash-flow classifications reconcile, and what is the exact accounting nature of residual funds parked in CC?\\n6. What are current connected-party guarantee utilization, reimbursement security and terms? Can co-guarantees be reduced?\\n7. What is official BSE liquidity over at least 20/60 sessions and the actual spread/restriction state? A 28,200-share secondary daily print does not answer this.\\n\\nSource register:\\n\\n- **ar2021**: [Original audit and CARO/IFC PDF29–41; financials42–44; FY20 comparative](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2020-21.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 020ce8d653f3c3aff6653aba5716702e0cfa768852f05c8bb21b5928e3221473.\\n- **ar2022**: [Original audit/CARO/IFC PDF29–44; financials45–47; notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2021-22.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 fbf36fb3268d9c491bd6ad2a806813298159fa5849f195cecfdd677e3daaad86.\\n- **ar2023**: [Original audit/CARO/IFC PDF31–46; financials47–49; notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2022-23.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 47e4f6d75f7b902be38f6930978039b5993fb3713a39b1140893b6883da391b3.\\n- **ar2024**: [Original audit/CARO/IFC PDF41–57; financials58–60; notes](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-for-F.Y.2023-24.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 ba3bf059e96e6d7725e9c276f564616b6a11f7eec79e1a592be7aea5a9f29ac9.\\n- **ar2025**: [Original standalone audit/CARO/IFC printed71–77; CFS80; consolidated109–136](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2024-25-Final.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 c468d7035eed77387c8034bd5de5da847196493f206ec16801f168413a51d71c.\\n- **ar2026**: [Revised FY26 report: printed29 BESS;77–78 objects;118–129 standalone audit/CARO/IFC;192–201 consolidated audit/IFC;202–207 statements;226–245 notes;249–265 RPT/transition](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Annual-Report-Rajesh-Power-2025-26-Final.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 3dae50c85009f5369a7f9403c9f09bfd6a3c5e802552c63d20352d23e696d3be.\\n- **prospectus**: [28 November 2024 prospectus; printed55–57 restated standalone statements; risk factors; objects; share capital](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Prospectus_RPSL.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 c3858e57fc7f46647f70197228082fb95983dd84006fd2b0844661bfbd54c3cf.\\n- **results2026**: [23 April 2026 audited results, Ind AS comparatives](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Audited-Standalone-Consolidated-Financial-Results-March-2026.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 18b32699958fe3e52bb6eeb6731dcbce816bebdaab0529397599d518ebdef4f9.\\n- **presentation2026**: [23 April 2026 investor presentation pp8–10/21–24, operational delivery and historical summaries](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Investor-Presentation-RPSL-H2FY26.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 6c89f782d0dc1a2310ccf0f1d77fcaf7e5111c0d2403191303f22447b6e5a30f.\\n- **transcript2026**: [23 April 2026 call pp8–10 collections,17–22 BESS and finance,31–32 returns](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Transcription-H2FY26-Earnings-Call-on-23.04.2026.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 ad4aecdd27d8febde69bfaac7ecbd91af2327b80c675e8b43b3719f2a28f90b7.\\n- **share2026**: [31 March 2026 statutory shareholding pattern, promoter and encumbrance declarations](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Shareholding%20Pattern%20-%2031.03.2026.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 58b24c22f940c58be8c992e90c88599eeabc35240cca1e95d9540a946a33d570.\\n- **bess2026**: [6 March 2026 issuer BESS update](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/20260306_%20Press%20Release%20for%20BESS%20update_.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 ee343b22f987e18096c0f4060d85dea907031152aeeb53b98266781c1018e51a.\\n- **bessaward**: [Issuer BESS award press release](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/Press%20Release%20_%20BESS%20_%20GUVNL.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 f551f7985dd6e3f2234dc87fb4c565dab99335d6b31e39d64cafbdc0a3743eeb.\\n- **nzsub**: [New Zealand wholly-owned subsidiary incorporated/confirmed 14 August 2026; filing dated 15 August 2026; nil turnover and no operations at disclosure](https://www.rajeshpower.com/img/New%20Foreign%20Subsidairy%20Company.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23. SHA-256 1136598cbf81102f359591ce225d8cdb06e969d42fffe8d5f8d6bc065a1ec892.\\n- **rating2026**: [Standalone rationale, liquidity, promoter quasi-equity adjustment, facility annexure](https://www.crisil.com/mnt/winshare/Ratings/RatingList/RatingDocs/RajeshPowerServicesLimited_July%2014_%202026_RR_399781.html). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **market_secondary**: [19 October close821.85, volume28200; corroborated StockAnalysis and Goodreturns](https://www.equitypandit.com/historical-data/544291). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **market_crosscheck**: [19 October close821.85, volume28200; postcutoff rows excluded](https://stockanalysis.com/quote/bom/544291/history/). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **security**: [Issuer-only master row: ISIN, shares18007392, lot100,SM; no inference of NSE traded liquidity](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **agm_secondary**: [All AGM resolutions reportedly approved; primary attachment retrieval failed so not counted as confirmed financing](https://bazaarwatch.com/announcement/141592/rajesh-power-services-ltd-scrutinizer-report-and-e-voting-results-for-annual-general-meeting-held-on). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **orders_secondary**: [October12/14 MGVCL471.92 and UGVCL69.004crore awards including taxes; not added mechanically to March/June backlog](https://www.whalesbook.com/company/announcements/RAJESH). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **bess_rfs**: [Original GUVNL Phase VII RfS mirrored document, 124 pages; sections 8–12, 19, 23: availability, efficiency, augmentation, delay, VGF and bank guarantees](https://solarquarter.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/1753286163254.pdf). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **peer_kec**: [Dated FY25 consolidated revenue, margin, NWC and business mix; not a current valuation comparable](https://www.kecrpg.com/press-release/earnings/financial-performance-for-quarter-%26-year-ended-31-march-2025-26-05-25). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **peer_transrail**: [Primary business navigation and annual-report index: T&D EPC and manufacturing comparison](https://transrail.in/investors/financial-reports/annual-reports/). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n- **peer_kpil**: [Exact corporate issuer primary investor index; no unverified numerical peer multiple](https://www.kalpataruprojects.com/investors/financials/annual-reports). Retrieved 2026-10-23.\\n\\nReconstruction: both compact and full packages contain every authored input and standard-library-only deterministic code required to regenerate all outputs. Compact omits bulky generated rows, not reconstruction inputs; full also includes canonical all-path output, valuation rows and the cash-event CSV for immediate inspection. Each package is independently tested after deletion of generated outputs and attestations. Reported archive verification refers to fresh downloaded bytes and regenerated outputs, not only a local cached copy.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","1422","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1471","4467","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","4505","12211","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","12235","14222","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","14253","16374","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","16403","18162","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","18205","22440","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","22481","25234","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","25260","31294","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","31333","41287","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","41326","43548","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","43576","44529","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","44552","46781","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","46830","47800","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","47845","56708","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"scope\"","\"audit_evidence.json\"","\"fa19a009881b53fa0f0f38c45f0e45d4693b5dd834d3a0c068d15a17a5902f7e\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"dffff08f58b185aad12b7ee1895520c3e8166abf48b0a71fd37e7437cab6bd21\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"cash_event_ledger.csv\"","\"b395b838aacee2d5df5f6c7199b7cd79330b0040689c60292b95bca4d7fac36e\"","\"Exact UTF-8 CSV text\"","\"3712a5d84fcfa258c90cf9d9ddfa9ff8ba9f0256df45cbda0a5a960e963078c3\"","\"factor_evidence.json\"","\"748e18a452a5b6024bc95a41b5625edc33a33d0268